const O={};globalThis.P=(en,ar)=>({en,ar});try{globalThis["D"]=O["D"]=({"sites":[{"id":"qalat-al-bahrain","n":"Qal'at al-Bahrain","na":"قلعة البحرين","c":"archaeological","s":"Dilmun capital · World Heritage","lat":26.2336,"lng":50.5206,"p":"Dilmun to Portuguese periods","dr":"c. 2300 BC – 16th century AD","el":"Tell 12 m high · 17.5 ha","ic":"wall","era":"P","t0":4325,"t1":400,"te":"The city under the fort","ta":"المدينة تحت القلعة","se":"Seven layers of a capital, from Dilmun to the Portuguese","sa":"سبع طبقات لعاصمة، من دلمون إلى البرتغاليين","desc":"A 12 m high tell on the north coast, 6 km from Manama, with seven stratified occupation layers from about 2300 BC to the 16th century AD. Regarded as the capital of Dilmun, it holds a Dilmun palace with warehouses, a Kassite palace, residential quarters on 12 m wide streets, city walls and gates, and the Portuguese fort on top that gave the site its name. About 25% of the 17.5 ha site has been excavated. Inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage List in 2005.","sig":"The longest continuous archaeological sequence in the Gulf and the only Dilmun-period city excavated at scale. About 50 cuneiform tablets were found, three from the reign of the Kassite king Agum.","ex":"Danish expedition (Geoffrey Bibby), 1954–1972; French CNRS team from the 1970s; Danish–Bahraini teams since 1987","disc":["Dilmun palace and warehouse complex of the late 3rd millennium BC","Kassite palace with cuneiform tablets of king Agum","City walls, gates and streets 12 m wide","Portuguese fort of the 16th century on the summit"],"refs":["Kervran, M., Hiebert, F. T. & Rougeulle, A. (2005). Qal'at al-Bahrain: a trading and military outpost, 3rd millennium B.C.–17th century A.D. Brepols.","Crawford, H. & Rice, M. (2000). Traces of Paradise: the archaeology of Bahrain 2500 BC–300 AD. I.B. Tauris.","UNESCO World Heritage Centre. Qal'at al-Bahrain – Ancient Harbour and Capital of Dilmun (ref. 1192), inscribed 2005."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"barbar-temple","n":"Barbar Temple","na":"معبد باربار","c":"archaeological","s":"Dilmun temple complex","lat":26.2262,"lng":50.484,"p":"Early Dilmun","dr":"c. 3000 – 2000 BC","el":"Three superimposed temples","ic":"platform","era":"P","t0":5025,"t1":3900,"te":"The temple of sweet water","ta":"معبد الماء العذب","se":"Three temples built over a sacred spring to the god of fresh water","sa":"ثلاثة معابد بُنيت فوق عين مقدسة لإله الماء العذب","desc":"Three temples built one above the other at the village of Barbar. Temple I (c. 3000 BC) stood on a platform about 25 by 16–18 m of local stone; Temple II, about 500 years later, enlarged the terrace in limestone; Temple III (2100–2000 BC) was the largest and stayed in use into the early 2nd millennium BC. A natural spring filled a pool in a subterranean shrine, read as the abzu of Enki, god of wisdom and fresh water, with channels carrying water to fields and gardens. Limestone blocks are thought to have been quarried on Jidda Island and brought by boat.","sig":"The clearest expression of Dilmun's water cult and one of the oldest temple sequences in the Gulf. The copper bull's head from Temple II is the emblem of the Bahrain National Museum.","ex":"Danish expedition (P. V. Glob; H. Andersen and P. Mortensen), 1954–1962; work resumed 2004","disc":["Copper bull's head from Temple II","Subterranean spring shrine with stone-lined pool","Three construction phases spanning a thousand years"],"refs":["Andersen, H. H. & Højlund, F. (2003). The Barbar Temples. Jutland Archaeological Society.","Højlund, F. (2005). New excavations at the Barbar Temple, Bahrain. Arabian Archaeology and Epigraphy 16.","Mortensen, P. (1971). On the date of the Barbar Temple in Bahrain. Artibus Asiae.","Al Halwachi, H. (2020). The copper bull's head at Barbar temple: chemical composition and casting alloy. Journal of Archaeological Science: Reports 32."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"saar-settlement","n":"Saar Settlement and Temple","na":"مستوطنة سار ومعبدها","c":"archaeological","s":"Early Dilmun town","lat":26.1967,"lng":50.4856,"p":"Early Dilmun","dr":"c. 2050 – 1800 BC","el":"c. 2.5 ha","ic":"jar","era":"P","t0":4075,"t1":3825,"te":"A town that kept its seals","ta":"بلدة احتفظت بأختامها","se":"Several hundred stamp seals from the houses of one small Dilmun town","sa":"مئات الأختام من بيوت بلدة دلمونية صغيرة","desc":"A planned Early Dilmun town of about 2.5 ha west of Manama, with houses along streets and a temple believed to have marked the summer solstice. Found in a 1977 survey and excavated 1977–1979 under M. Ibrahim, then by the London–Bahrain Archaeological Expedition in 1990–1999. Finds include copper fishhooks, bitumen imported from Mesopotamia, pearl-oyster shell and seed pearls, and several hundred stamp seals, four or five in a single house; 48 more came from the adjoining burial ground.","sig":"The most complete plan of an ordinary Dilmun town and the richest seal assemblage from domestic contexts anywhere in the Gulf.","ex":"London–Bahrain Archaeological Expedition (R. Killick, J. Moon, H. Crawford), 1990–1999","disc":["Several hundred Early Dilmun stamp seals","Temple aligned to the summer solstice","Copper fishhooks and imported bitumen"],"refs":["Crawford, H., Killick, R. & Moon, J. (eds.) (1997). The Dilmun Temple at Saar. Kegan Paul International.","Killick, R. & Moon, J. (eds.) (2005). The Early Dilmun Settlement at Saar. Archaeology International.","Crawford, H. (1997). The site of Saar: Dilmun reconsidered. Antiquity 71, 701–708."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"ain-umm-sujoor","n":"Ain Umm es-Sujur (Diraz)","na":"عين أم السجور","c":"archaeological","s":"Dilmun spring and well shrine","lat":26.2218,"lng":50.4701,"p":"Early Dilmun","dr":"3rd millennium BC","el":"Oval hollow c. 60 × 30 m","ic":"well","era":"P","t0":4525,"t1":3525,"te":"The well that was a temple","ta":"البئر التي كانت معبدًا","se":"Stone-built wells at a spring, treated as holy places","sa":"آبار مبنية بالحجر عند عين، عوملت كأماكن مقدسة","desc":"A spring site in Diraz whose name means 'mother of the spring of overflowing waters'. An oval hollow about 60 by 30 m holds stone-built wells dated to the 3rd millennium BC, with rooms and ovens added later on the south side. First dug by the Danish expedition in 1954; a Rikkyo University (Japan) team in the 1990s identified three wells and was the first to interpret them as holy places. The northern well is known as the water temple.","sig":"Links the Dilmun water cult of Barbar to the island's spring line; one of the few shrines built directly on a spring.","ex":"Danish expedition 1954; Rikkyo University expedition, 1990s","disc":["Three stone-lined wells of the 3rd millennium BC","Later rooms and ovens on the southern side"],"refs":["Matsumoto, K. & Oguchi, K. (2001). Expeditions and discoveries in West Asia by Japanese archaeologists. Orient 36(1), 7–33.","Bibby, G. (1969). Looking for Dilmun. Knopf."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"aali-royal-mounds","n":"A'ali Royal Mounds","na":"تلال عالي الملكية","c":"archaeological","s":"Royal tumuli of Early Dilmun · World Heritage","lat":26.1497,"lng":50.5128,"p":"Early Dilmun","dr":"c. 2050 – 1750 BC","el":"Up to 50 m across and 12 m high","ic":"mound","era":"P","t0":4075,"t1":3775,"te":"Where the kings of Dilmun lie","ta":"حيث يرقد ملوك دلمون","se":"Seventeen giant mounds in one village, the tombs of Dilmun’s first kings","sa":"سبعة عشر تلًا عملاقًا في قرية واحدة، مدافن أوائل ملوك دلمون","desc":"Seventeen royal mounds (13 single and two pairs) in the village of A'ali, up to 50 m in diameter and 12 m high, built between about 2050 and 1750 BC when Dilmun was ruled by kings. Chieftain mounds of 13–26 m lie around them. Three cuneiform inscriptions were found in Mound 8. The royal mounds form 15 of the 21 components of the Dilmun Burial Mounds World Heritage property inscribed in 2019.","sig":"The earliest monumental royal tombs in Eastern Arabia and the main evidence for the emergence of kingship in Dilmun.","ex":"Bahrain Authority for Culture and Antiquities with Moesgaard Museum (S. T. Laursen), 2010s","disc":["Royal mounds up to 50 m wide and 12 m high","Cuneiform inscriptions from Mound 8","Chieftain mounds of 13–26 m diameter"],"refs":["Laursen, S. T. (2017). The Royal Mounds of A'ali in Bahrain: the emergence of kingship in Early Dilmun. Aarhus University Press.","Bahrain Authority for Culture and Antiquities. Typology of Dilmun burial mounds (culture.gov.bh).","UNESCO World Heritage Centre. Dilmun Burial Mounds (ref. 1542), inscribed 2019."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"hamad-town-moundfields","n":"Hamad Town and Janabiyah Mound Fields","na":"حقول تلال مدينة حمد والجنبية","c":"archaeological","s":"Dilmun burial mound fields · World Heritage","lat":26.118,"lng":50.495,"p":"Early Dilmun","dr":"c. 2200 – 1750 BC","el":"Fields of some dozen to several thousand tumuli","ic":"mound","era":"P","t0":4225,"t1":3775,"te":"A landscape of the dead","ta":"مشهد للموتى","se":"Tens of thousands of small mounds once covered the north of the island","sa":"عشرات الآلاف من التلال الصغيرة غطّت يومًا شمال الجزيرة","desc":"Mound fields west and south of A'ali that make up six of the 21 components of the Dilmun Burial Mounds property, about 11,774 mounds in all. Early Type mounds (c. 2200–2050 BC) average 2.5 m across and under 1.5 m high; about 1,000 survive of an estimated 17,000. Late Type mounds (c. 2050–1750 BC) are 6–11 m across and 2–3 m high; mapping suggested 58,000 once existed, with about 13,500 remaining. Each mound covers a central stone chamber inside a low ring wall; Janabiyah and Madinat Hamad 2 hold mounds with subsidiary burials.","sig":"One of the densest Bronze Age cemetery landscapes known anywhere, and a record of how a whole island population buried its dead for four centuries.","ex":"Danish excavations from 1954; Bahrain National Museum survey and mapping projects","disc":["Early Type mounds of 2.5 m average diameter","Late Type mounds 6–11 m across with ring walls","Mounds with subsidiary burials at Janabiyah"],"refs":["Højlund, F. (2007). The Burial Mounds of Bahrain: social complexity in Early Dilmun. Jutland Archaeological Society.","Bahrain Authority for Culture and Antiquities. Typology of Dilmun burial mounds (culture.gov.bh).","UNESCO World Heritage Centre. Dilmun Burial Mounds (ref. 1542), inscribed 2019."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"shakhura-necropolis","n":"Shakhura Necropolis","na":"مدافن الشاخورة","c":"archaeological","s":"Tylos-period burial mound","lat":26.201,"lng":50.524,"p":"Tylos (Hellenistic to Parthian)","dr":"3rd century BC – 4th century AD","el":"Main mound c. 80 m long","ic":"jar","era":"H","t0":2275,"t1":1625,"te":"Feasts for the dead","ta":"ولائم للموتى","se":"Ninety graves in one mound, and the ashes of funeral banquets","sa":"تسعون قبرًا في تل واحد، ورماد ولائم الجنازة","desc":"A burial mound about 80 m long in the village of Shakhura, used from the 3rd century BC to the 4th century AD, when Bahrain was known as Tylos. Excavations led by Captain Robert Higham in the 1960s found 90 graves, the earliest at the centre and later ones added in arcs, with Roman and Parthian artefacts including an intact ribbed glass bowl now in the British Museum. A 2008 excavation found an ash layer with glazed pottery, fish and animal bones, read as the remains of funeral banquets. The mound was largely demolished in the 1990s.","sig":"A key site for the Tylos period, when the island traded with the Hellenistic and Parthian worlds.","ex":"R. Higham, 1960s; Bahrain National Museum, 2008","disc":["Intact ribbed glass bowl of Roman date","Ash layer with food remains from funeral banquets"],"refs":["Crawford, H. & Rice, M. (2000). Traces of Paradise: the archaeology of Bahrain 2500 BC–300 AD. I.B. Tauris.","Salles, J.-F. & Lombard, P. (eds.). Les fouilles françaises de Abu Saiba: données nouvelles sur la phase Tylos de Bahreïn. Proceedings of the Seminar for Arabian Studies."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"khamis-mosque","n":"Al-Khamis Mosque","na":"مسجد الخميس","c":"archaeological","s":"Early Islamic mosque with twin minarets","lat":26.2083,"lng":50.5483,"p":"Islamic (Uyunid to Usfurid)","dr":"11th – 15th century AD (traditionally 692 AD)","el":"Building 22.8 × 20 m","ic":"platform","era":"H","t0":950,"t1":550,"te":"Two minarets, two centuries apart","ta":"مئذنتان بينهما قرنان","se":"The oldest standing mosque in Bahrain, rebuilt twice","sa":"أقدم مسجد قائم في البحرين، أُعيد بناؤه مرتين","desc":"Traditionally founded in 692 AD, but an inscription points to an 11th-century foundation under the Uyunids, when it had one minaret; the second minaret was added about two centuries later under the Usfurids. The mosque was rebuilt in the 14th and 15th centuries: an early prayer hall with a flat roof on wooden columns, and a later section on arches and masonry piers dated to 1339. A 12th-century limestone mihrab slab carrying Qur'anic verses was found during restoration. Recent excavations by the Early Islamic Bahrain Project place it within Bilad al-Qadim, the main centre of settlement in the 11th–13th centuries.","sig":"The principal surviving monument of medieval Bahrain and the anchor of the Bilad al-Qadim heritage trail.","ex":"Early Islamic Bahrain Project (T. Insoll, University of Exeter) with the Bahrain Authority for Culture and Antiquities","disc":["12th-century mihrab slab with Qur'anic inscription","Prayer hall section dated 1339"],"refs":["Insoll, T., Almahari, S., MacLean, R. et al. (2016). Bilad al-Qadim revisited: recent archaeological research at the Al-Khamis Mosque, Ain Abu Zaydan and Abu Anbra, Bahrain. Arabian Archaeology and Epigraphy.","Petersen, A. (1996). Dictionary of Islamic Architecture. Routledge."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"bilad-al-qadim","n":"Bilad al-Qadim","na":"بلاد القديم","c":"archaeological","s":"Medieval Islamic settlement","lat":26.211,"lng":50.553,"p":"Islamic","dr":"11th – 13th century AD","el":"North-east of Awal island","ic":"map","era":"H","t0":975,"t1":725,"te":"The old town","ta":"البلد القديم","se":"The main town of the island when Manama did not yet exist","sa":"البلدة الرئيسية في الجزيرة قبل أن توجد المنامة","desc":"An area of Islamic-period settlement in the north-east of the main island, the main centre of settlement in the 11th–13th centuries AD, named 'the old town' after Manama grew beside it. Excavations by the Anglo-Bahraini Early Islamic Bahrain Project at the Al-Khamis Mosque, the spring of Ain Abu Zaydan and the Abu Anbra cemetery have traced houses, water systems and burials of the medieval town.","sig":"The only medieval Islamic town in the Gulf islands studied over two decades of excavation; a bridge between Dilmun-era springs and the modern capital.","ex":"Early Islamic Bahrain Project (University of Manchester, then Exeter; T. Insoll), 2001 onwards","disc":["Medieval houses and water channels at Ain Abu Zaydan","Abu Anbra cemetery"],"refs":["Insoll, T. (2005). The Land of Enki in the Islamic Era: pearls, palms and religious identity in Bahrain. Kegan Paul.","Insoll, T., Almahari, S., MacLean, R. et al. (2016). Bilad al-Qadim revisited. Arabian Archaeology and Epigraphy."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"arad-fort","n":"Arad Fort","na":"قلعة عراد","c":"archaeological","s":"Islamic coastal fort","lat":26.2525,"lng":50.6275,"p":"Late Islamic","dr":"Late 15th – early 16th century AD","el":"Square plan with four round towers","ic":"wall","era":"H","t0":530,"t1":200,"te":"The fort on its own island","ta":"القلعة على جزيرتها","se":"A square fort that once stood on an islet, now joined to Muharraq","sa":"قلعة مربعة كانت على جزيرة صغيرة، التحمت اليوم بالمحرق","desc":"A square fort with a cylindrical tower at each corner, built in the late 15th or early 16th century, before the Portuguese occupation of 1622; a Portuguese map of about 1635 shows it under siege with inner and outer walls. A trench once filled from wells surrounded it, and nose-shaped openings for marksmen pierce the upper walls. Restored in 1984–1987 under architect Archie Walls using sea stone, lime, sand and palm trunks, without cement. The fort stood on a separate islet before land reclamation joined it to Muharraq.","sig":"The best-preserved Islamic-period fort in Bahrain and a model restoration in traditional materials.","ex":"Restoration by A. G. Walls, Directorate of Tourism and Archaeology, 1984–1987","disc":["Portuguese siege map of c. 1635","Restoration without cement, 1984–1987"],"refs":["Walls, A. G. (1987). Arad Fort, Bahrain: its restoration, its history and defences. Directorate of Tourism and Archaeology, Bahrain.","Walls, A. G. (2004). Arabian mud brick technology: some thoughts after the Bam earthquake. Construction History Society Newsletter 69, 11–19."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"riffa-fort","n":"Riffa Fort","na":"قلعة الرفاع","c":"archaeological","s":"19th-century fort on the escarpment","lat":26.1152,"lng":50.5565,"p":"Modern","dr":"Rebuilt 1812 – 1818","el":"On the cliff over the Hunanaiya valley","ic":"wall","era":"H","t0":320,"t1":200,"te":"A house on the cliff","ta":"بيت على الجرف","se":"Rebuilt in 1812 on an older foundation, home of the ruling family","sa":"أُعيد بناؤها عام 1812 على أساس أقدم، وكانت مسكن الأسرة الحاكمة","desc":"A fort on the cliff between East and West Riffa, first built in the early 18th century by Fareer bin Rahhal and rebuilt on its foundations in 1812–1818 by Sheikh Salman bin Ahmed Al Fateh Al Khalifa, who used it as a residence. Sheikh Isa bin Ali Al Khalifa, ruler from 1869 to 1932, was born here. The fort was restored in 1993 and overlooks the desert valley cut into the island's interior escarpment.","sig":"Marks the move of the ruling family's seat inland in the early 19th century and sits on the island's geological rim.","ex":"Bahrain Authority for Culture and Antiquities (restoration 1993)","disc":["Early 18th-century foundation beneath the 1812 fort","Birthplace of Sheikh Isa bin Ali Al Khalifa"],"refs":["Bahrain Authority for Culture and Antiquities. Sheikh Salman bin Ahmed Al Fateh Fort (bahrain.com).","Bahrain News Agency. قلعة الرفاع بنيت عام 1812م."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"pearling-path","n":"Pearling Path and Bu Maher Fort","na":"طريق اللؤلؤ وقلعة بو ماهر","c":"archaeological","s":"Pearling heritage · World Heritage","lat":26.2413,"lng":50.6135,"p":"Pearling economy","dr":"2nd century AD – 1930s","el":"3.5 km trail · 17 buildings · 3 oyster beds","ic":"boat","era":"H","t0":1900,"t1":90,"te":"The road to the oyster beds","ta":"الطريق إلى مغاصات اللؤلؤ","se":"Seventeen houses, a fort and three oyster beds that made an island economy","sa":"سبعة عشر بيتًا وقلعة وثلاث مغاصات صنعت اقتصاد جزيرة","desc":"A 3.5 km path through Muharraq linking 17 historic buildings, from the seafront Bu Maher fort on the island's southern tip to the Siyadi complex, together with three oyster beds in the northern waters. Pearl diving in the Gulf is mentioned in Assyrian texts of about 2000 BC; the economy that these buildings record ran from the 2nd century to the 1930s, with a golden age from the 1850s to 1930, before oil was found in 1932 and cultured pearls ended the trade. Inscribed on the World Heritage List on 30 June 2012.","sig":"The last complete example of the cultural tradition of pearling that shaped the Gulf for two millennia.","ex":"Bahrain Authority for Culture and Antiquities; Pearling Path visitor centre (Aga Khan Award 2019)","disc":["Bu Maher fort at the departure point of the pearling fleet","Three oyster beds (hayrat) in the northern waters"],"refs":["UNESCO World Heritage Centre. Pearling, Testimony of an Island Economy (ref. 1364rev), inscribed 2012.","Faroughy, A. (1951). The Bahrein Islands (750–1951). Verry, Fisher & Co."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"jebel-dukhan","n":"Jebel Dukhan","na":"جبل الدخان","c":"geological","s":"Crest of the Bahrain anticline","lat":26.0333,"lng":50.55,"p":"Eocene","dr":"c. 56 – 34 million years ago","el":"134.1 m, the island's highest point","ic":"cliff","era":"D","t0":56000000.0,"t1":34000000.0,"te":"The mountain of smoke","ta":"جبل الدخان","se":"The highest point of a slow dome of Eocene limestone","sa":"أعلى نقطة في قبة بطيئة من حجر الإيوسين الجيري","desc":"The island's highest point at 134.1 m, near its geographic centre, on the axis of the north–south Bahrain anticline. The fold is simple, with no surface faults; subsurface faults are normal tension faults with throws usually under 15 m. The core exposes the lower Eocene Rus Formation (about 67 m thick, chalky dolomitic limestone with chert and anhydrite), overlain by the Dammam Formation, whose rimrock of Eocene limestone encircles the central basin in cliffs more than 30 m high. Bahrain's first oil well was drilled at its foot in 1932.","sig":"The structural heart of Bahrain: the anticline that traps the island's oil and gas and lifts its oldest rocks to the surface.","ex":"U.S. Geological Survey / Bahrain Petroleum Company (Willis 1967)","disc":["Rus Formation: 67 m of chalky limestone with anhydrite","Dammam rimrock cliffs over 30 m high","Anticline without surface faults"],"refs":["Willis, R. P. (1967). Geology of the Arabian Peninsula: Bahrain. U.S. Geological Survey Professional Paper 560-E."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"first-oil-well","n":"First Oil Well and the Bahrain Field","na":"أول بئر نفط وحقل البحرين","c":"geological","s":"Oil discovery of 1932","lat":26.0394,"lng":50.548,"p":"Petroleum geology","dr":"1931 – present","el":"Reservoirs 380 – 2,000 m deep","ic":"oil","era":"N","t0":95,"t1":0,"te":"Where the Gulf’s oil age began","ta":"حيث بدأ عصر النفط في الخليج","se":"Oil first spurted below Jebel Dukhan on 16 October 1931","sa":"تدفق النفط أول مرة أسفل جبل الدخان في 16 أكتوبر 1931","desc":"Well No. 1 below Jebel Dukhan, where oil first spurted on 16 October 1931 and flowed from 2 June 1932: the first oil found on the Arabian side of the Gulf. Frank Holmes had won a concession in 1925 after first finding fresh water for the ruler; the Bahrain Petroleum Company (Bapco) was set up in 1929 by Standard Oil of California. The Awali (now Bahrain) field covers about 400 km², roughly 80% of the main island, with oil reservoirs between 380 and 2,000 m and gas to nearly 4,000 m. Output peaked at about 80,000 barrels a day in 1971; gas was found in 1948 and the offshore Abu Safah field in 1963.","sig":"The discovery that started the Gulf oil industry, drilled on the anticline that geology had already revealed at the surface.","ex":"Bahrain Petroleum Company (Bapco), nationalised 1980","disc":["First oil on the Arabian side of the Gulf, 1931–1932","Field of 400 km² under 80% of the island"],"refs":["Archer, S. (2012). Bahrain: 80 years and still producing. GEO ExPro.","Vassiliou, M. (2009). Historical Dictionary of the Petroleum Industry. Scarecrow Press."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"interior-basin","n":"Interior Basin and Rimrock Escarpment","na":"الحوض الداخلي والجرف الحلقي","c":"geological","s":"Eroded core of the anticline","lat":26.07,"lng":50.52,"p":"Eocene to Quaternary","dr":"Rocks 56 – 34 million years old; landform Quaternary","el":"Cliffs over 30 m","ic":"cliff","era":"D","t0":2580000.0,"t1":0,"te":"The bowl in the middle of the island","ta":"الحوض في قلب الجزيرة","se":"A ring of limestone cliffs around a hollow where the dome was eaten away","sa":"حلقة من الجروف الجيرية حول تجويف أُكلت فيه القبة","desc":"A closed basin in the centre of the island, ringed by a rimrock of Eocene Dammam limestone standing in cliffs more than 30 m high. Erosion cut through the crest of the Bahrain anticline, removing the younger beds and exposing the Rus Formation inside the ring; the basin is the only place with meaningful local recharge of groundwater. The Dammam Formation has four members here: the 'shark's-tooth shale' (9–15 m), a brown crystalline limestone (about 33 m), an orange marl (6–20 m) and a white limestone (6–62 m).","sig":"A textbook breached anticline, and the landscape that explains Bahrain's springs, quarries and oil in one view.","ex":"U.S. Geological Survey (Willis 1967)","disc":["Breached anticline with inward-facing cliffs","Four members of the Dammam Formation exposed"],"refs":["Willis, R. P. (1967). Geology of the Arabian Peninsula: Bahrain. U.S. Geological Survey Professional Paper 560-E."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"sharks-tooth-shale","n":"Shark's-Tooth Shale","na":"طفل أسنان القرش","c":"geological","s":"Fossil bed of the Dammam Formation","lat":26.055,"lng":50.57,"p":"Lower to middle Eocene","dr":"c. 50 million years ago","el":"9 – 15 m thick","ic":"fossil","era":"D","t0":50000000.0,"t1":45000000.0,"te":"Teeth from a vanished sea","ta":"أسنان من بحر زال","se":"A grey shale full of shark teeth, laid down when Bahrain was sea floor","sa":"طفل رمادي مليء بأسنان القرش، ترسّب حين كانت البحرين قاع بحر","desc":"The lowest member of the Dammam Formation on Bahrain: 9.1–15.2 m of grey to yellow shale and marl named for its abundant fossil shark teeth, exposed on the inner slopes of the rimrock. It records the shallow Eocene sea that covered the region about 50 million years ago, before the Tertiary limestones were uplifted on the anticline.","sig":"The island's most accessible fossil bed and a marker horizon used to map the whole Dammam Formation across eastern Arabia.","ex":"U.S. Geological Survey (Willis 1967)","disc":["Fossil shark teeth in a 9–15 m marl and shale bed"],"refs":["Willis, R. P. (1967). Geology of the Arabian Peninsula: Bahrain. U.S. Geological Survey Professional Paper 560-E."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"dammam-aquifer-springs","n":"Dammam Aquifer and the Springs","na":"خزان الدمام والعيون","c":"geological","s":"Artesian springs of the land of two seas","lat":26.196,"lng":50.556,"p":"Holocene to present","dr":"Springs used for over 4,000 years","el":"Ain Adhari, Adhari Park","ic":"river","era":"N","t0":4525,"t1":0,"te":"The water that made Bahrain","ta":"الماء الذي صنع البحرين","se":"Fresh water from Saudi Arabia rises through the island’s limestone","sa":"ماء عذب من السعودية يصعد عبر حجر الجزيرة الجيري","desc":"Bahrain's springs rise from the Dammam Formation's Khobar and Alat limestones, recharged where the beds outcrop in Saudi Arabia; the Khobar member supplies over 75% of groundwater. Ain Adhari, south-west of Manama, was described as a river of fast water about three metres wide feeding the date groves. Abstraction from the Dammam rose from about 63 million m³ in 1952 to 188 million m³ in 2001, about 68% above the estimated inflow of 112 million m³, and the saline front advanced 60–139 m a year; most springs, including Adhari, no longer flow naturally and almost 70% of usable groundwater has been lost or salinised.","sig":"The hydrogeology behind Dilmun's temples, the medieval towns and the island's name, and a warning written in salt.","ex":"Bahrain Ministry of Works; University of Plymouth (Al-Noaimi 2004)","disc":["Khobar limestone yields over 75% of groundwater","Abstraction 68% above inflow by 2001","Saline front moving 60–139 m per year"],"refs":["Al-Noaimi, M. A. (2004). Development of water resources in Bahrain: a combined approach of supply–demand analysis. PhD thesis, University of Plymouth.","Willis, R. P. (1967). Geology of the Arabian Peninsula: Bahrain. USGS Professional Paper 560-E.","News of Bahrain. The land of two seas: the freshwater springs that made Bahrain."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"submarine-springs","n":"Submarine Freshwater Springs","na":"العيون البحرية","c":"geological","s":"Fresh water rising from the sea floor","lat":26.3,"lng":50.64,"p":"Holocene to present","dr":"Recorded 1829 – 20th century","el":"About 3 fathoms (5.5 m) depth","ic":"wave","era":"N","t0":4525,"t1":0,"te":"Sweet water under salt","ta":"ماء عذب تحت الملح","se":"Divers filled goatskins from springs on the sea bed, especially off Muharraq","sa":"غاصوا ليملأوا القِرب من عيون في قاع البحر، خصوصًا قبالة المحرق","desc":"Fresh water from the same Eocene aquifer that feeds the land springs also rises through the sea floor around the islands, most numerously off Muharraq. An 1829 British traveller described a spring in about three fathoms of water; divers fitted a jar over the spring mouth and filled goatskin bags, and pearling crews used the springs to take on water at sea. The phenomenon is the origin of the name 'Bahrain', the two seas of salt and sweet water. Location shown is approximate.","sig":"A rare hydrogeological phenomenon that shaped a pearling economy and a country's name.","ex":"Historical accounts (1829; early 20th-century British gazetteer)","disc":["Springs in about 5 m of water off Muharraq","Goatskin bags filled by divers for pearling fleets"],"refs":["News of Bahrain. The land of two seas: the freshwater springs that made Bahrain.","Al-Noaimi, M. A. (2004). Development of water resources in Bahrain. PhD thesis, University of Plymouth."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"ras-al-barr-sabkha","n":"Ras al-Barr Sabkha","na":"سبخة رأس البر","c":"geological","s":"Coastal salt flats of the south","lat":25.83,"lng":50.55,"p":"Holocene","dr":"Last few thousand years","el":"Sea level, southern tip","ic":"salt","era":"D","t0":6000,"t1":0,"te":"Where the island turns to salt","ta":"حيث تتحول الجزيرة إلى ملح","se":"Beach sands and salt crusts on the island’s quiet southern tip","sa":"رمال شاطئية وقشور ملح على طرف الجزيرة الجنوبي الهادئ","desc":"Recent beach sands and sabkha (salt-flat) deposits concentrated at Ra's al Jaza'ir and Ra's al Barr in the south and south-west of the island, where the land is low and the Eocene aquifers carry water too saline for use. The sabkha surface forms as sea water and brine evaporate in the capillary zone, leaving gypsum and halite crusts; the sand gazelle and migrating waders use the surrounding flats.","sig":"The modern end of Bahrain's story: evaporite forming today on the same island whose Eocene rocks hold anhydrite from an older sea.","ex":"U.S. Geological Survey (Willis 1967)","disc":["Sabkha and beach deposits at Ras al Jazayir and Ras al Barr"],"refs":["Willis, R. P. (1967). Geology of the Arabian Peninsula: Bahrain. U.S. Geological Survey Professional Paper 560-E."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"hawar-islands","n":"Hawar Islands","na":"جزر حوار","c":"ecology","s":"Ramsar site · Socotra cormorant colony","lat":25.6667,"lng":50.8333,"p":"Living today","dr":"Ramsar site since 1997","el":"5,200 ha · 16 main islands","ic":"bird","era":"N","t0":30,"t1":0,"te":"Islands of a hundred thousand birds","ta":"جزر مئة ألف طائر","se":"The world’s largest breeding colony of Socotra cormorants, with dugongs and gazelles","sa":"أكبر مستعمرة تكاثر لغراب البحر السوقطري في العالم، مع الأطوم والغزلان","desc":"An archipelago of 16 main desert islands (36 in all) about 19 km south-east of the main island, with a highest point of 22 m; Hawar itself is over 41 km². Designated a Ramsar wetland of 5,200 ha on 27 October 1997 and a national protected area with access restricted by the coast guard. In 1992, 200,000–300,000 adult Socotra cormorants were recorded, the largest known breeding concentration in the world; greater flamingos and gulls winter in numbers, dugongs and sea turtles feed on the seagrass beds, and reintroduced Arabian oryx and sand gazelle live on the main island.","sig":"One of the most important seabird sites in the Middle East and the only part of Bahrain in near-pristine condition.","ex":"Hawar Islands Protected Area Management Plan (2003); BirdLife International","disc":["200,000–300,000 Socotra cormorants counted in 1992","Ramsar site no. 920, 5,200 ha","Reintroduced oryx and gazelle"],"refs":["Ramsar Sites Information Service. Hawar Islands (site 920), designated 1997.","BirdLife International (2024). Important Bird Area factsheet: Hawar Islands.","IUCN (2004). Evaluation of the Hawar Islands World Heritage nomination."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"tubli-bay","n":"Tubli Bay and Ras Sanad Mangroves","na":"خليج توبلي وقرم رأس سند","c":"ecology","s":"Ramsar site · last mangroves","lat":26.1732,"lng":50.5722,"p":"Living today","dr":"Protected by Royal Decree 2006","el":"1,350 ha","ic":"mangrove","era":"N","t0":30,"t1":0,"te":"The last mangroves","ta":"آخر أشجار القرم","se":"A shrinking bay that still feeds thousands of wintering shorebirds","sa":"خليج يتقلص لكنه ما زال يطعم آلاف الطيور الشاطئية الشاتية","desc":"A shallow bay of 1,350 ha between Manama and Sitra, protected by Royal Decree in 2006 and listed as a Ramsar wetland. The Avicennia marina stand at Ras Sanad in its south-west corner is Bahrain's last natural mangrove and a wildlife reserve. It is the country's most important site for migrating and wintering waterbirds, with counts of 2,000 black-headed gulls, 1,000 little stints, 300 ringed plovers and 250 ruddy turnstones. Since 1975 most of the original mangrove, intertidal and date-palm habitats have been lost to reclamation, roads and sewage, especially on the northern shore.","sig":"A living remnant of the mangrove coast that once lined the island, and a measure of what reclamation costs.","ex":"Supreme Council for Environment; BirdLife International (Key Biodiversity Area 8017)","disc":["Last natural Avicennia mangrove in Bahrain","Counts of up to 2,000 black-headed gulls"],"refs":["BirdLife International. Key Biodiversity Area factsheet: Tubli Bay (KBA 8017).","Ramsar Sites Information Service. Tubli Bay (site 921)."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"al-areen","n":"Al Areen Wildlife Park and Reserve","na":"محمية العرين","c":"ecology","s":"Captive-breeding reserve for Arabian wildlife","lat":26.0156,"lng":50.4956,"p":"Living today","dr":"Founded 1976","el":"8 km² in two halves","ic":"gazelle","era":"N","t0":50,"t1":0,"te":"Where the oryx came back","ta":"حيث عاد المها","se":"A fenced reserve that bred the Arabian oryx and gazelle for release on Hawar","sa":"محمية مسيّجة ربّت المها العربي والغزال لإطلاقهما في حوار","desc":"A fenced desert reserve of about 8 km² at Sakhir on the west side of the island, founded in 1976 and divided into a public park and a protected reserve with two surface reservoirs. It holds indigenous plants and animals plus exotic species from Africa and Asia, with 82 bird species and more than 45 animal species recorded; its flagship animals are the Arabian oryx, once extinct in the wild, and the reem (sand) gazelle. Oryx and gazelle bred here were reintroduced to the Hawar Islands.","sig":"The source population for Bahrain's reintroduced large mammals and its main environmental education site.","ex":"Supreme Council for Environment / Al Areen Wildlife Park","disc":["Arabian oryx breeding herd","Oryx and gazelle reintroduced to Hawar"],"refs":["Bahrain Clearing-House Mechanism (CBD). Al-Areen Wildlife Park and Reserve.","Oxford Business Group (2011). The Report: Bahrain 2011."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"fasht-al-adhm","n":"Fasht Al Adhm Coral Reef","na":"فشت العظم","c":"ecology","s":"Largest coral platform of Bahrain","lat":26.25,"lng":50.8,"p":"Living today","dr":"Bleaching 1996, 1998, 2017","el":"c. 200 km² reef area","ic":"crab","era":"N","t0":30,"t1":0,"te":"A reef at the edge of endurance","ta":"شعاب على حافة الاحتمال","se":"Corals here survive 37 °C summers, then lose almost everything in a bad year","sa":"تصمد الشعاب هنا أمام صيف بحرارة 37 درجة، ثم تخسر كل شيء تقريبًا في سنة سيئة","desc":"A reef platform of about 200 km² east of the main island; Bahrain's reef areas (fashts) total about 850 km², more than its land area. In the 1980s about 30 coral species were recorded, with Acropora cover of 50–90% in 1988; mass bleaching in 1996 (37.7 °C) and 1998 (38 °C) hit Bahrain harder than any other Gulf country, and the 2017 event, above 34 °C from July to October, cut nearshore hard-coral cover from 13.6% to 1.4%. The survivors are massive Platygyra, Favia and Porites; branching corals are essentially gone, and the nearshore carbonate budget has fallen to near zero.","sig":"Among the world's most heat-tolerant reefs, and a front line of climate change in the Gulf.","ex":"AlMealla, Edullantes & Hepburn, University of Essex / Bahrain (2024)","disc":["Coral cover 50–90% in 1988, over 90% lost within years","Nearshore hard coral down to 1.4% after 2017"],"refs":["AlMealla, R. K., Edullantes, B. & Hepburn, L. J. (2024). Bleaching threatens positive carbonate budgets on Bahraini reefs. Marine Biology 171, 39."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"gulf-of-salwa-dugongs","n":"Dugongs of the Gulf of Salwa","na":"أطوم خليج سلوى","c":"ecology","s":"Second-largest dugong population","lat":25.75,"lng":50.72,"p":"Living today","dr":"Surveys 1986 – 2016","el":"Waters under 10 m deep","ic":"dolphin","era":"N","t0":40,"t1":0,"te":"The sea cows of the shallows","ta":"أبقار البحر في الضحال","se":"Herds of several hundred dugongs graze the seagrass between Bahrain and Qatar","sa":"قطعان من مئات الأطوم ترعى الأعشاب البحرية بين البحرين وقطر","desc":"The shallow waters south of Bahrain, mostly under 10 m deep with extensive Halodule and Halophila seagrass, hold the world's second-largest dugong population after Australia. A herd of 674 was counted in March 1986, with calves 15.7% of the group; winter surveys in 2004 recorded groups of 577 and 97 between Bahrain and Qatar, and 508 animals were counted in under 1 km² in 2015–2016. Bahrain surveys in 2006 gave estimates similar to those of twenty years earlier. The area is an Important Marine Mammal Area of 10,676 km² that also holds humpback and bottlenose dolphins and finless porpoises.","sig":"A globally important population of a vulnerable species, dependent on the same seagrass that feeds Hawar's turtles.","ex":"Preen (1989, 2004); Hodgson (2009); Marshall et al. (2018)","disc":["Herd of 674 dugongs counted in 1986","508 animals in under 1 km² in 2015–2016"],"refs":["Preen, A. (2004). Distribution, abundance and conservation status of dugongs and dolphins in the southern and western Arabian Gulf. Biological Conservation 118, 205–218.","Marshall, C. D. et al. (2018). Large dugong aggregations persist in coastal Qatar. Marine Mammal Science 34(4), 1154–1163.","IUCN Marine Mammal Protected Areas Task Force. Gulf of Salwa IMMA factsheet."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"tree-of-life","n":"Tree of Life","na":"شجرة الحياة","c":"ecology","s":"Lone mesquite in the southern desert","lat":25.9941,"lng":50.5832,"p":"Living today","dr":"Planted c. 1582","el":"9.75 m tall · 6 km from Jebel Dukhan","ic":"plant","era":"N","t0":444,"t1":0,"te":"The tree with no spring","ta":"الشجرة بلا عين","se":"A 400-year-old tree alive in bare desert, its water still unexplained","sa":"شجرة عمرها 400 عام حيّة في صحراء جرداء، وماؤها ما زال لغزًا","desc":"A Prosopis (mesquite) tree 9.75 m tall standing alone on a low hill about 6 km from Jebel Dukhan and 40 km from Manama. A 1990s soil and tree-ring study dated its planting to 1582. Its roots may reach 50 m down to groundwater, though no spring is visible; other explanations invoke moisture held in the sand. Archaeologists in October 2010 unearthed 500-year-old pottery and other artefacts beside it, and about 65,000 people visit each year.","sig":"A living test of the island's hidden water, and the most visited natural site in Bahrain.","ex":"Bahrain Authority for Culture and Antiquities (2010 excavation)","disc":["Planting dated to 1582 by tree-ring and soil analysis","500-year-old pottery found beside the tree in 2010"],"refs":["Mancuso, S. (2020). The Incredible Journey of Plants. Other Press.","Toumi, H. (2009). Bahrain's Tree of Life ranks high on Seven Wonders list. Gulf News."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"diraz-temple","n":"Diraz Temple","na":"معبد الدراز","c":"archaeological","s":"Dilmun columned temple · partly excavated","lat":26.2171,"lng":50.4746,"p":"Early Dilmun","dr":"3rd – early 2nd millennium BC","el":"Columns 120 cm across · 700 m² surviving","ic":"platform","era":"P","t0":4525,"t1":2725,"te":"The hall of nine columns","ta":"قاعة الأعمدة التسعة","se":"A columned Dilmun temple beside the Budaiya Highway, unlike anything at Barbar or in Mesopotamia","sa":"معبد دلموني ذو أعمدة قرب طريق البديع، لا يشبه معبد باربار ولا معابد بلاد الرافدين","desc":"Only about 700 m² survive, in the south-western corner of an original settlement just north of the Budaiya Highway in Diraz. A central hall held two rows of cylindrical columns about 120 cm in diameter, of which nine remain standing up to 60 cm high, spaced 2 to 3 m apart, with a square base between them read as an altar or statue base and a small square cella to the east. A British expedition partly explored the site in 1973–75 with the Bahrain Directorate of Archaeology, finding Barbar-type pottery, a snake vessel and two Dilmun stamp seals with animal figures; the finds date it to the 3rd or early 2nd millennium BC. East of the temple a subterranean tomb of the Kassite period stayed in use into the Iron Age.","sig":"Its column architecture differs from both Mesopotamian temples and the nearby Barbar temple, so it records a separate strand of Dilmun religious building.","ex":"British Archaeological Expedition with the Bahrain Directorate of Archaeology, 1973–75","disc":["Nine cylindrical columns about 120 cm across","Two Dilmun stamp seals and a snake vessel","Kassite tomb reused into the Iron Age"],"refs":["Bahrain Authority for Culture and Antiquities. Diraz Temple (cultural tourism destinations page).","Wikipedia contributors (2026). Diraz Temple, citing the excavation reports of the British Archaeological Mission."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"al-markh","n":"Al Markh Ubaid-period Site","na":"موقع تل المرخ","c":"archaeological","s":"Late Stone Age fishing camp · Ubaid pottery","lat":25.99,"lng":50.48,"p":"Late Ubaid / Neolithic","dr":"c. 5000 – 3800 BC","el":"6,896 flint pieces","ic":"shell","era":"P","t0":7025,"t1":5800,"te":"Fishers on a higher sea","ta":"صيادون على بحر أعلى","se":"A sand-covered mound in the south-west where Stone Age fishers left painted Ubaid sherds and thousands of flints","sa":"تل مغطى بالرمل في الجنوب الغربي ترك فيه صيادو العصر الحجري كسر فخار عبيدي ملوّن وآلاف الأدوات الصوانية","desc":"A low, sand-covered mound about 6 km south of Zallaq and 1,200 m from the present shore. Ubaid-period sherds were noticed in 1971 and a British team under Michael Roaf excavated it in 1975, recovering 6,896 flint pieces, mainly scrapers, with pottery dated to about 3800 BC. The earlier phase holds painted late or post-Ubaid sherds that were mended and reused, bones of sparid fish, pearl oyster and other shellfish, and shallow fire pits; the later phase has little pottery, more flint, larger fish, and the first bones of goat, hare and dugong, which were common. Sea level in the late Neolithic is thought to have stood up to 4 m higher, so the mound may once have been an islet used seasonally by fishers and hunters.","sig":"The main Ubaid-related site in Bahrain and one of the earliest records of dugong hunting in the Gulf.","ex":"Michael Roaf (British expedition), 1975","disc":["Painted Ubaid sherds mended and reused","Dugong bones common in the later phase","Evidence of sea level up to 4 m higher than today"],"refs":["Roaf, M. (1976). Excavations at Al Markh, Bahrain. Proceedings of the Seminar for Arabian Studies 6.","Beech, M. J. (2010). Mermaids of the Arabian Gulf: archaeological evidence for the exploitation of dugongs from prehistory to the present. Liwa 2(3), 3–18.","Wikipedia contributors (2026). Al Markh, citing Rice (1994) and Thuesen (1989)."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"wadi-al-sail-mounds","n":"Wadi al-Sail Burial Mounds","na":"مدافن وادي السيل","c":"archaeological","s":"Earliest Dilmun cairn graveyard · Japanese excavations","lat":26.1,"lng":50.53,"p":"Early Dilmun (Riffa type)","dr":"c. 2300 – 1900 BC","el":"Wadi 4 km long · hundreds of mounds","ic":"mound","era":"P","t0":4325,"t1":3925,"te":"Where the mound-building began","ta":"حيث بدأ بناء المدافن","se":"Hundreds of early cairn mounds along a wadi on the plateau, radiocarbon-dated to about 2300 BC","sa":"مئات المدافن الركامية المبكرة على جانبي وادٍ في الهضبة، مؤرخة بالكربون المشع إلى نحو 2300 قبل الميلاد","desc":"A wadi about 4 km long on the inland plateau, running from south-east to north-west, with hundreds of 'Riffa type' burial mounds along its slopes, mainly in the upper 3 km; mounds of this type were once scattered around Riffa but were mostly lost to building, so those in the wadi are the survivors. A Kanazawa University mission led by Takeshi Gotoh excavated 15 mounds in the northern part over five seasons from 2015 to 2019, while the Bahrain Authority for Culture and Antiquities dug 11 more; 266 possible mounds were identified there. All were cairn burials, and eight radiocarbon dates fall between 2300 and 1900 BC, so construction probably began around 2300 BC or earlier. Some graves held Mesopotamian and Umm an-Nar pottery and stone beads, evidence of maritime trade by the end of the 3rd millennium BC.","sig":"The earliest phase of the Dilmun mound tradition, showing the rise of a tribal society before the royal mounds of A'ali.","ex":"Japanese Archaeological Mission (Kanazawa University: T. Gotoh, K. Saito, M. Abe, A. Uesugi) with the Bahrain Authority for Culture and Antiquities, 2015–2019","disc":["Eight radiocarbon dates of 2300–1900 BC","Umm an-Nar and Mesopotamian pottery in the graves","266 possible mounds in the northern sector alone"],"refs":["Gotoh, T., Saito, K., Abe, M. & Uesugi, A. (2020). Excavations at Wādī al-Sail, Bahrain 2015–2019. Proceedings of the Seminar for Arabian Studies 50, 171–187.","Abe, M. & Uesugi, A. (2021). Reconsidering the date of Riffa type burial mounds in the Early Dilmun period: new radiocarbon data from Wadi al-Sail, Bahrain. Al-Rāfidān 42, 75–85.","Bahrain Authority for Culture and Antiquities (2019). Field visit and lecture on the Wadi al-Sail excavations (news release, February 2019)."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"al-hajjar-cemetery","n":"Al-Hajjar Cemetery","na":"مقبرة الحجر","c":"archaeological","s":"Rock-cut graves used for 2,000 years","lat":26.21,"lng":50.51,"p":"Early Dilmun to Tylos","dr":"c. 2000 BC – 300 AD","el":"Four phases of use over two millennia","ic":"jar","era":"P","t0":4025,"t1":1725,"te":"Graves cut into the rock","ta":"قبور منحوتة في الصخر","se":"Underground rock-cut tombs reused from Dilmun to Tylos times, where the first Dilmun seal in a grave was found","sa":"مدافن منحوتة تحت الأرض أُعيد استخدامها من دلمون إلى تايلوس، وفيها وُجد أول ختم دلموني داخل قبر","desc":"A cemetery on the edge of the Budaiya Highway at the village of Al-Hajjar, explored mainly by the Bahrain Department of Antiquities in 1970–71 and again in the mid-1990s. Its northern complex of underground rock-cut graves, some still sealed with flat capstones, is rare in Bahrain; it was used in the Early Dilmun period (c. 2000–1800 BC), under the Kassites (c. 1400–1300 BC), in Late Dilmun times (c. 900–800 BC) and in the Tylos period (c. 200 BC – 300 AD), and the southern part holds unexcavated Tylos mounds. In 1970 the first Early Dilmun stamp seal ever found in a grave was recovered near a skeleton's neck. Later offerings include soft-stone and alabaster vessels, stamp and cylinder seals, glass, iron weapons, jewellery and two limestone statues unique in Bahrain, now in the Bahrain National Museum.","sig":"The clearest case in Bahrain of one burial ground reused across four cultural periods, from Dilmun to the Hellenistic-Parthian world.","ex":"Bahrain Department of Antiquities, 1970–71 and mid-1990s","disc":["First Early Dilmun stamp seal found in a grave (1970)","Two limestone statues unique in Bahrain","Kassite and Late Dilmun reuse of the tombs"],"refs":["Bahrain Authority for Culture and Antiquities. Al-Hajjar (cultural tourism destinations page).","Wikipedia contributors (2026). Al Hajar, Bahrain, citing the Los Angeles Times (29 May 1988) on relics found in the village."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"abu-saiba-tylos","n":"Abu Saiba Tylos Cemetery","na":"تل أبو صيبع","c":"archaeological","s":"Tylos hill cemetery · French excavations","lat":26.21,"lng":50.49,"p":"Tylos (Hellenistic to Parthian)","dr":"c. 200 BC – 300 AD","el":"Mound 70 m across · 4–4.5 m high","ic":"jar","era":"H","t0":2225,"t1":1725,"te":"Dice from the age of Tylos","ta":"نرد من عصر تايلوس","se":"A circular burial hill 8 km west of Manama with nearly a hundred built graves of the Tylos age","sa":"تل مدافن دائري على بعد 8 كم غرب المنامة فيه نحو مئة قبر مبني من عصر تايلوس","desc":"A circular mound about 70 m across and 4 to 4.5 m high, 8 km west of Manama and 500 m south of the Janusan roundabout on the Budaiya road. A Bahraini team dug briefly in 1983, finding glazed pottery, glass, jewellery and tombstones now in the Tylos hall of the Bahrain National Museum; since 2017 the French Archaeological Mission under Pierre Lombard has worked with the Bahrain Authority for Culture and Antiquities. By 2021 nearly 70 built graves including three burial jars had been revealed over about 1,000 m², and by March 2022 excavation of about a third of the hill had uncovered 93 burials, 23 of them new that season, with three intact tombs holding glass, bronze, beads and pottery. The 2022 season also produced a pair of dice, the first found in Bahrain, read as part of a game or a fortune-telling ritual of the middle Tylos period (50 BC – 150 AD).","sig":"One of more than 30 Tylos cemeteries in the Shakhura–Abu Saiba–Maqaba area, and the one being excavated with modern methods today.","ex":"French Archaeological Mission (Pierre Lombard) with the Bahrain Authority for Culture and Antiquities, since 2017; Bahraini excavation 1983","disc":["First pair of ancient dice found in Bahrain (2022)","93 burials uncovered by 2022, including children in jars","Intact tombs 43–46 with glass, bronze and beads"],"refs":["Bahrain Authority for Culture and Antiquities (2021). Results of the 2021 season at Abu Saiba (news release, 7 April 2021).","Aldroubi, M. (2022). Ancient dice discovered in Bahrain may have been used for fortune telling. The National, 22 March 2022.","Bahrain Authority for Culture and Antiquities. Shakhura archaeological site (cultural tourism destinations page)."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"samahij-christian-building","n":"Samahij Christian Building","na":"المبنى المسيحي في سماهيج","c":"archaeological","s":"Earliest Christian building in the Gulf · excavated 2019–23","lat":26.28,"lng":50.63,"p":"Late Antique to Early Islamic","dr":"Mid-4th – mid-8th century AD","el":"Eight rooms with plaster floors","ic":"platform","era":"H","t0":1675,"t1":1275,"te":"The bishop under the mosque","ta":"الأسقف تحت المسجد","se":"Under a cemetery mound in Muharraq lay an eight-room building with plaster crosses, perhaps the residence of the bishop of Mashmahig","sa":"تحت تل مقبرة في المحرق مبنى من ثماني غرف بصلبان جصية، ربما كان مقر أسقف مشماهيج","desc":"Found under a mound in the village cemetery of Samahij on Muharraq, beneath a later mosque that helped preserve it. Excavated from 2019 to 2023 by Timothy Insoll of the University of Exeter and Salman Almahari of the Bahrain Authority for Culture and Antiquities, the stone building has plastered walls and floors and eight surviving rooms, including a kitchen with hearths made from amphora bases, a refectory, a workroom and three living rooms. Radiocarbon dates place its use between the mid-4th and mid-8th centuries AD, after which it was abandoned as the population became Muslim. Finds include three plaster crosses, graffiti of a fish and part of a Chi-Rho, about a dozen copper coins probably Sasanian, carnelian beads and Indian pottery, small wine glasses, spindle whorls and copper needles, and remains of pork, fish and shellfish. It may have been the residence of the bishop of the diocese recorded as Meshmahig in Church of the East sources, which mention a bishop excommunicated in 410.","sig":"The first physical evidence of the Church of the East in Bahrain and probably the oldest Christian building known in the Arabian Gulf.","ex":"University of Exeter (T. Insoll) and Bahrain Authority for Culture and Antiquities (S. Almahari), 2019–2023","disc":["Three plaster crosses and a Chi-Rho graffito","Radiocarbon dates of the mid-4th to mid-8th century","Carnelian beads and Indian pottery showing trade with India"],"refs":["University of Exeter (2024). Archaeologists discover the first Christian building in Bahrain (news release, 12 July 2024).","Arab News (2024). Archaeologists in Bahrain unearth Gulf's earliest Christian structure.","Emirates News Agency (WAM) (2024). Bahraini, British archaeologists discover first Christian building in Bahrain dated to 4th century CE."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"al-sayah-island","n":"Al Sayah Island","na":"جزيرة السياح","c":"archaeological","s":"Man-made island around a sea spring · National Heritage List","lat":26.26,"lng":50.6,"p":"Early Islamic to pearling era","dr":"7th – 8th century AD onward","el":"Walls enclosing an islet under 20 m, later 40 m wide","ic":"well","era":"H","t0":1400,"t1":100,"te":"An island built around a spring","ta":"جزيرة بُنيت حول عين","se":"Walls and coral platforms built in the sea around a freshwater spring so pearling boats could take on water","sa":"جدران ومنصات مرجانية بُنيت في البحر حول عين عذبة ليتزود منها الغواصون بالماء","desc":"A small islet north-west of Muharraq, between Busaiteen and Bahrain Bay, long thought natural. Excavations from January 2022 by a British team under Robert Carter with the Bahrain Authority for Culture and Antiquities, the first systematic marine and underwater archaeology survey in Bahrain, showed it was built in the sea around a freshwater spring of the kind local people call a kawkab or chochab. A circular wall enclosed a cistern around the spring, forming an island under 20 m wide; a later curved wall widened it to about 40 m, straight walls enclosed rectangular cabins, and large coral blocks formed a platform more than 60 m long, with a small building holding a water-raising mechanism beside the spring. Pottery of the 7th–8th centuries AD lies mixed with piles of pearl oysters that cover most of the island, and water was drawn for boats anchored along the quay. Threatened by a road project in 2022, it was placed on the National Heritage List.","sig":"Described by Salman Al-Mahari as one of the oldest examples of sea filling anywhere, and a direct link between the island's sea springs and its pearling fleet.","ex":"Bahrain Authority for Culture and Antiquities with a British team led by Robert Carter, from 2022","disc":["Shown in 2022 to be man-made, at least 1,200 years old","Pearl-oyster shell heaps over 7th–8th century pottery","Coral-block platform more than 60 m long"],"refs":["Altuntaş, L. (2022). British archaeologists unearth the 1,200-year-old man-made island. Arkeonews, 13 February 2022.","Gulf Daily News (2024). Historic Al Sayah Island to get new lease of life, 26 May 2024.","Afikra Daftar (2026). How we remember water: on Ain Adhari, Al Sayah Island, and the afterlife of Bahrain's springs, citing Insoll et al. (2025)."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"rus-formation-outcrop","n":"Rus Formation Outcrop (Sakhir Racecourse)","na":"مكشف تكوين الرس (سباق الخيل)","c":"geological","s":"Early Eocene dolostone · studied 2023","lat":26.0725,"lng":50.522,"p":"Rus Formation, Early Eocene","dr":"c. 56 – 48 million years ago","el":"26.5 m section over 1.6 km²","ic":"fossil","era":"D","t0":56000000,"t1":48000000,"te":"A lagoon turned to stone","ta":"بحيرة ساحلية تحجّرت","se":"Chalky dolostone with chert nodules and geodes, laid down in lagoons and tidal channels of a narrow Eocene strait","sa":"دولوستون طباشيري بعقد الصوان والجيودات، ترسب في بحيرات ساحلية وقنوات مدّية لمضيق إيوسيني ضيق","desc":"An outcrop of about 1.6 km² at the Horse Racing Club in central Bahrain, on the northern edge of the doubly plunging anticline, where a 26.5 m section of the Rus Formation is exposed; boreholes show the formation thickens to about 125 m in the west and 73 m in the east of the island. A 2023 study by Aimen Amer and colleagues logged 13 facies of dolostone, dolomitic marl and claystone with chert nodules near the top and calcite geodes in several beds; XRD showed 88% of 58 samples to be mainly dolomite, and only quartz pseudomorphs after gypsum and thin halite laminae remain of the evaporites. The beds record restricted lagoons, sabkha pools, tidal flats and channels and storm washovers along a shoreline trending NNW–SSE, with wind-blown quartz sand probably from the Arabian Shield. The authors argue that the Rus here is not mainly evaporitic and question its role as a regional hydrocarbon seal.","sig":"The most detailed published section of the Rus Formation in Bahrain, the unit that holds the island's lowest aquifer and the geodes found across the interior.","ex":"University of Turku (Amer, Skyttä, Ojala, Heilimo), published 2023","disc":["13 facies in a 26.5 m section","88% of samples predominantly dolomite","Evidence of a narrow storm-swept Eocene strait"],"refs":["Amer, A., Skyttä, P., Ojala, A. & Heilimo, E. (2023). New insights into the depositional facies of the Early Eocene Rus Formation, Bahrain. Marine and Petroleum Geology 154, 106338.","Willis, R. P. (1963). Geology of the Arabian Peninsula: Bahrain. USGS Professional Paper 560-E."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"jidda-island","n":"Jidda Island","na":"جزيرة جدة","c":"geological","s":"Limestone islet · quarry of the Barbar temple","lat":26.19,"lng":50.41,"p":"Limestone islet of the western shelf","dr":"Quarried c. 2500 BC","el":"0.5 km² · cliffs to 10 m","ic":"island","era":"P","t0":4525,"t1":0,"te":"The quarry across the water","ta":"المحجر عبر الماء","se":"A cliff-edged islet west of Bahrain whose stone is believed to have been shipped to build the Barbar temple","sa":"جزيرة محاطة بجروف غرب البحرين يُعتقد أن حجرها نُقل بالقوارب لبناء معبد باربار","desc":"Three small islets about 17.5 km west of Manama, just north of Umm an Nasan, with a total area of 0.5 km² and a highest point of 10 m; the main island consists of limestone cliffs. Rock blocks are believed to have been cut here and carried across the water to build the Barbar temple on the north coast of Bahrain Island, whose second temple was walled with carved quarried stone. The island is joined to Umm an Nasan by a 1.75 km causeway, is uninhabited and is closed to the public.","sig":"The likely source of the dressed limestone of Bahrain's most important Dilmun temple, and a sample of the low limestone islets of the western shelf.","ex":"No published geological survey; quarry link proposed by the Danish Barbar excavators","disc":["Believed source of the Barbar temple stone","Limestone cliffs rising to 10 m"],"refs":["Wikipedia contributors (2026). Jidda Island.","Madain Project. Barbar Temple, citing Bibby (1969), Andersen & Højlund (2003) and Rice (1990)."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"causeway-pleistocene","n":"Pleistocene Shelf of the King Fahd Causeway","na":"رصيف البليستوسين عند جسر الملك فهد","c":"geological","s":"Ice-age sea-level record under the causeway","lat":26.17,"lng":50.44,"p":"Pleistocene to Holocene","dr":"c. 110,000 – 6,000 years ago","el":"Dolomite up to 40 m · cover 3–9 m","ic":"wave","era":"D","t0":110000,"t1":6000,"te":"When the Gulf ran dry","ta":"حين جفّ الخليج","se":"Boreholes for the causeway cut through dolomite and sands recording the emptying and refilling of the Arabian Gulf","sa":"اخترقت حفريات الجسر دولوميت ورمالًا تسجل جفاف الخليج العربي وامتلاءه من جديد","desc":"Sediments studied along the King Fahd Causeway between Bahrain and Saudi Arabia by Ata Darwish and Curtis Conley (1990). A lower dolomite interval up to 40 m thick is built of repeated cycles of skeletal packstone, mudstone, pelletal grainstone and green mudstone, dated to the mid-Pleistocene before the first great sea-level fall of 110,000–90,000 years ago. Above it a 3–9 m interval of quartz sandstone, skeletal grainstone, quartz sand and aragonitic oolitic sand records a transgression of 90,000–75,000 years ago, a late Pleistocene blanket of dune sand as aridity grew, and the Flandrian flooding of the Gulf about 6,000 years ago. During the lowstand the whole Gulf was emptied and the sediments were leached, cemented and dolomitised; in wetter periods fresh water dissolved aragonite and precipitated calcite.","sig":"A measured record of how the shallow Gulf floor between Bahrain and Arabia dried out and flooded with the ice ages.","ex":"Darwish & Conley (King Abdulaziz University), 1990; recent sands noted by the USGS survey of 1963","disc":["Gulf emptied entirely in the late Pleistocene regression","Reflooded about 6,000 years ago"],"refs":["Darwish, A. & Conley, C. D. (1990). Pleistocene-Holocene sedimentation and diagenesis along the King Fahd causeway between Saudi Arabia and Bahrain. Journal of King Abdulaziz University: Earth Sciences 3(1), 63–79.","Willis, R. P. (1963). Geology of the Arabian Peninsula: Bahrain. USGS Professional Paper 560-E."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"mashtan-island","n":"Mashtan Island","na":"جزيرة مشتان","c":"ecology","s":"Marine protected area since 2002 · seagrass nursery","lat":25.95,"lng":50.55,"p":"Living today","dr":"Protected by Order No. 1 of 2002","el":"c. 2.5 km² island · 2.5 km offshore","ic":"island","era":"N","t0":25,"t1":0,"te":"A nursery in the seagrass","ta":"حضانة في الأعشاب البحرية","se":"A low island off the south-east coast whose seagrass beds feed dugongs and turtles and shelter young shrimp and fish","sa":"جزيرة منخفضة قبالة الساحل الجنوبي الشرقي تغذي أعشابها البحرية الأبقار البحرية والسلاحف وتحمي صغار الروبيان والأسماك","desc":"A small low island about 2.5 km off the south-east coast of the main island, listed by Bahrain's biodiversity clearing-house at about 2.5 km² and protected under National Commission for Wildlife Protection Order No. 1 of 2002. Its sandy beaches are ringed by extensive seagrass beds and reefs; the seagrass is an important feeding area for dugongs and sea turtles and a protected nursery ground for shrimp and finfish, and the island is also recognised as an Important Bird Area. Bahrain's seagrass comprises three species, Halodule uninervis, Halophila stipulacea and Halophila ovalis, with the largest beds running from south of Fasht al-Adhm to the Hawar Islands.","sig":"One of only a handful of marine protected areas in Bahrain and the nearest to the main island's dugong and turtle feeding grounds.","ex":"Supreme Council for Environment (formerly National Commission for Wildlife Protection)","disc":["Protected in 2002","Feeding ground for dugongs and sea turtles","Nursery for shrimp and finfish"],"refs":["Kingdom of Bahrain Clearing-House Mechanism (CBD). Mashtan Island (protected areas page).","Naser, H. A. (2016). Management of marine protected zones – case study of Bahrain, Arabian Gulf. In Applied Studies of Coastal and Marine Environments, IntechOpen.","Sea Alarm Foundation (2026). Country wildlife response profile: Bahrain."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"dohat-arad","n":"Dohat Arad Mangrove Reserve","na":"محمية دوحة عراد","c":"ecology","s":"Planted mangroves · marine protected area 2003","lat":26.25,"lng":50.65,"p":"Living today","dr":"Protected by Order No. 4 of 2003","el":"0.5 km² sheltered bay","ic":"mangrove","era":"N","t0":25,"t1":0,"te":"New mangroves in an old bay","ta":"قرم جديد في خليج قديم","se":"A sheltered mudflat bay on Muharraq where mangroves have been replanted for waders and young fish","sa":"خليج طيني محمي في المحرق أُعيد فيه غرس القرم للطيور الخواضة وصغار الأسماك","desc":"A sheltered bay of about 0.5 km² on the eastern side of Muharraq Island, about 3 km from Muharraq town, dominated by mudflats. It was declared a marine protected area in 2003 by Order No. 4 of the Public Commission for the Protection of Marine Resources, Environment and Wildlife, as a site for mangrove restoration and wildlife habitat, and a mangrove reserve has been planted on its north-east shore. The bay is an important resting and feeding area for resident and migratory waders and shelters juvenile fish; the greater flamingo is present year-round on Bahrain's tidal flats. Bahrain's only natural mangrove stand, of Avicennia marina, survives at Tubli Bay, which makes the Arad planting the island's second mangrove site.","sig":"Bahrain's main mangrove restoration site and a protected feeding ground for shorebirds beside the country's airport island.","ex":"Supreme Council for Environment (formerly PCPMREW)","disc":["Protected in 2003 for mangrove restoration","Resting and feeding area for migratory waders"],"refs":["Naser, H. A. (2016). Management of marine protected zones – case study of Bahrain, Arabian Gulf. In Applied Studies of Coastal and Marine Environments, IntechOpen.","Sea Alarm Foundation (2026). Country wildlife response profile: Bahrain.","International Parks. Dohat Arad, Bahrain (protected area profile)."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"maqabah-hypocolius","n":"Maqabah Hypocolius Roost","na":"مرقد البُلبُل الرمادي في مقابة","c":"ecology","s":"Important Bird Area · grey hypocolius roost","lat":26.2033,"lng":50.4875,"p":"Living today","dr":"Assessed as an IBA in 1994","el":"0.157 km² of date-palm scrub","ic":"bird","era":"N","t0":35,"t1":0,"te":"The grey bird of the palm groves","ta":"الطائر الرمادي في بساتين النخيل","se":"An abandoned palm garden near Budaiya where Bahrain's wintering grey hypocolius gather to roost","sa":"بستان نخيل مهجور قرب البديع يتجمع فيه البُلبُل الرمادي الشاتي في البحرين للمبيت","desc":"A patch of 0.157 km² of abandoned, privately owned date-palm scrub and open ground at 2–5 m elevation in the village of Maqabah, about 10 km from Manama, bounded by roads, farms and housing. Scattered date palms cover about 60% of it. BirdLife International identified it as an Important Bird Area, last assessed in 1994, as the main roost in Bahrain of the grey hypocolius (Hypocolius ampelinus), a passage and wintering bird that feeds by day in palm gardens elsewhere on the island. It has no legal protection, though it has been proposed as a wildlife reserve, and faces cutting of palm scrub, disturbance of the roosts, rubbish and encroaching housing.","sig":"The one site in Bahrain tied to the grey hypocolius, a bird of a family found only in the Middle East, and a reminder of the vanished northern palm belt.","ex":"BirdLife International / Key Biodiversity Areas partnership","disc":["Main roost of the grey hypocolius in Bahrain","Only 0.157 km², with no formal protection"],"refs":["Key Biodiversity Areas Partnership. Maqabah (KBA factsheet 8018), BirdLife International.","Wikipedia contributors (2026). Muqaba, citing BirdLife International."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"spec-spring-line","n":"The Spring Line and the Dilmun Towns","c":"speculative","s":"Cross-category hypothesis","lat":26.215,"lng":50.51,"p":"Early Dilmun / Holocene","dr":"c. 2500–1800 BC (test window)","el":"","desc":"A connection between records on this map. Bahrain's springs rise from the Khobar and Alat limestones of the Dammam Formation, recharged in Saudi Arabia, and have been used for over 4,000 years. The Dilmun capital at Qal'at al-Bahrain, the Barbar temple built over a spring pool, the stone wells of Ain Umm es-Sujur and the town of Saar all lie within a few kilometres of each other on the north and north-west of the island. The idea is that the artesian spring line, where the aquifer first breaks the surface, fixed where Dilmun built its temples and towns, and that the Dilmun water cult was a map of that hydrogeology.","sig":"If true, the distribution of Early Dilmun sites would be predicted by the aquifer's outcrop and pressure head, which would help locate unexcavated settlements and explain why the island, not the mainland coast, became the centre of Dilmun.","ex":"","conf":"Untested AI speculation (connection between published records)","ev":"Dammam Aquifer and the Springs: springs rise from the Khobar member, which yields over 75% of groundwater, and have been used for over 4,000 years. Barbar Temple: a natural spring filled a pool in a subterranean shrine, with channels to fields and gardens, from c. 3000 BC. Ain Umm es-Sujur: three stone-built wells of the 3rd millennium BC on a spring, treated as holy places, about 1.5 km from Barbar. Qal'at al-Bahrain: the Dilmun capital 6 km from Manama on the north coast, occupied from c. 2300 BC. Saar: a planned Dilmun town about 3 km south of Barbar.","an":"Jericho (Tell es-Sultan, Jordan Valley) grew for more than ten thousand years beside the perennial spring of Ain es-Sultan, which Kathleen Kenyon's excavations showed to be the reason for its founding and persistence (Kenyon 1957). Oasis settlement tied to a single spring is a well-documented pattern.","val":"Map every Early Dilmun site against the outcrop of the Khobar and Alat members and the reconstructed 3rd-millennium potentiometric surface; date spring carbonates (tufa or travertine) at Barbar and Ain Umm es-Sujur by U-series; check whether the oldest phase of each site sits on a spring; compare with sites on the Saudi mainland coast.","disc":["Rival explanation 1: the north coast was chosen for its harbours and the sea route to Mesopotamia, and springs were a secondary convenience.","Rival explanation 2: date-palm agriculture on the best soils, not the springs themselves, drew settlement to the north.","Rival explanation 3: the spring line in the Bronze Age ran elsewhere, and the sites only appear to cluster on it because the modern springs are the ones we know."],"refs":["Al-Noaimi, M. A. (2004). Development of water resources in Bahrain: a combined approach of supply–demand analysis. PhD thesis, University of Plymouth.","Andersen, H. H. & Højlund, F. (2003). The Barbar Temples. Jutland Archaeological Society.","Matsumoto, K. & Oguchi, K. (2001). Expeditions and discoveries in West Asia by Japanese archaeologists. Orient 36(1), 7–33.","Kervran, M., Hiebert, F. T. & Rougeulle, A. (2005). Qal'at al-Bahrain: a trading and military outpost, 3rd millennium B.C.–17th century A.D. Brepols.","Kenyon, K. M. (1957). Digging up Jericho. Ernest Benn, London."],"ic":"spark","era":"?","te":"Did Dilmun Follow the Springs?","ta":"هل تبعت دلمون العيون؟","se":"Dilmun's temples and towns sit on the same few kilometres of coast where fresh water breaks the surface. Did the aquifer draw the map of Dilmun? Nobody has tested this yet.","sa":"تقع معابد دلمون ومدنها على الكيلومترات القليلة نفسها من الساحل حيث يخرج الماء العذب إلى السطح. هل رسم الخزان الجوفي خريطة دلمون؟ لم يختبر أحد ذلك بعد.","na":"خط العيون ومدن دلمون","t0":4525,"t1":3775,"spec":{"bas":"link","key":"spring-line","sty":"ai-spring-line","spot":{"en":"Born from a coincidence of places on this map. The Barbar temple, the wells of Ain Umm es-Sujur, the town of Saar and the capital at Qal'at al-Bahrain all lie within a few kilometres of each other, and two of them were built directly on springs. The spring record on the same map says the water has been used for over 4,000 years, the same age as the sites.","ar":"وُلدت الفكرة من تطابق أماكن على هذه الخريطة. معبد باربار وآبار عين أم السجور وبلدة سار والعاصمة في قلعة البحرين تقع كلها على بعد كيلومترات قليلة بعضها من بعض، واثنان منها بُنيا فوق عيون مباشرة. وسجل العيون على الخريطة نفسها يقول إن الماء استُعمل أكثر من 4,000 عام، وهو عمر المواقع نفسه."},"lk":[["dammam-aquifer-springs","Fresh water rising through the island's limestone for over 4,000 years","ماء عذب يصعد عبر حجر الجزيرة الجيري منذ أكثر من 4,000 عام"],["barbar-temple","A temple built over a spring pool, c. 3000 BC","معبد بُني فوق حوض عين، نحو 3000 ق.م"],["ain-umm-sujoor","Stone wells on a spring, treated as holy places","آبار حجرية على عين، عوملت كأماكن مقدسة"],["qalat-al-bahrain","The capital, on the same coast","العاصمة، على الساحل نفسه"]],"pred":{"en":"The oldest phase of every major Early Dilmun site will sit on or beside a spring whose carbonate deposits date to the 3rd millennium BC, and sites will be absent from stretches of coast where the aquifer did not then reach the surface.","ar":"ستقع أقدم مرحلة في كل موقع دلموني مبكر رئيسي فوق عين أو بجانبها، تعود رواسبها الكربونية إلى الألف الثالث قبل الميلاد، وستغيب المواقع عن أجزاء الساحل التي لم يكن الخزان يبلغ فيها السطح آنذاك."},"test":{"en":"Overlay the Early Dilmun sites on the aquifer outcrop and reconstructed pressure surface; date spring carbonates at Barbar and Diraz; excavate below the earliest floors to see whether a spring was already flowing.","ar":"وضع مواقع دلمون المبكرة فوق مكشف الخزان وسطح الضغط المعاد بناؤه، وتأريخ الرواسب الكربونية للعيون في باربار والدراز، والحفر تحت أقدم الأرضيات لمعرفة ما إذا كانت العين تجري آنذاك."},"wrong":{"en":"If major Dilmun sites turn out to lie far from any spring, or if the springs began flowing centuries after the sites were founded, the idea fails.","ar":"إذا تبين أن مواقع دلمون الرئيسية بعيدة عن أي عين، أو أن العيون بدأت تجري بعد تأسيس المواقع بقرون، تسقط الفكرة."}}},{"id":"spec-sea-spring-oysters","n":"Sea Springs and the Oyster Beds","c":"speculative","s":"Cross-category hypothesis","lat":26.29,"lng":50.63,"p":"Holocene / pearling economy","dr":"2nd century AD – 1930s (test window: present day)","el":"","desc":"A connection between records on this map. Fresh water from the Dammam aquifer rises through the sea floor around the islands, most numerously off Muharraq, where an 1829 account describes a spring in about three fathoms and divers filling goatskins. The three oyster beds (hayrat) of the Pearling Path lie in the same northern waters, and the man-made island of Al Sayah was built around a sea spring and is covered in pearl-oyster shell. The idea is that submarine groundwater discharge, carrying nutrients and lowering salinity, made these particular banks the richest pearl-oyster grounds, so that the fleet, the fort of Bu Maher and the built island all followed the springs.","sig":"If true, the pearl wealth of Bahrain would be a direct product of its hydrogeology, the oyster beds could be mapped from groundwater discharge, and the decline of the springs since the 1950s would be a measurable threat to the beds themselves.","ex":"","conf":"Untested AI speculation (connection between published records)","ev":"Submarine Freshwater Springs: springs in about 5 m of water, most numerous off Muharraq, used by pearling crews to take on water at sea. Pearling Path: three oyster beds in the northern waters, a fleet departing from Bu Maher fort at the southern tip of Muharraq. Al Sayah Island: built in the sea around a freshwater spring north-west of Muharraq, with pearl-oyster shell heaps over 7th–8th century pottery. Dammam Aquifer: the same Khobar and Alat limestones feed the land springs; abstraction 68% above inflow by 2001 and most land springs no longer flow.","an":"Johannes (1980) showed that submarine groundwater discharge delivers nutrients to coastal waters and shapes benthic communities, with documented cases from Western Australia and Hawaii; Moore (2010) reviewed how this discharge, traced with radon and radium, controls productivity along many coasts.","val":"Survey the three hayrat and the waters off Muharraq with radon-222 and salinity profiling to map submarine discharge; compare oyster density and condition on banks with and without discharge; test whether the oyster beds sit on the same Khobar outcrop as the springs; use historical spring positions from the 1829 account and the British gazetteer.","disc":["Rival explanation 1: the oyster beds are controlled by water depth, hard substrate and currents, and the springs merely lie in the same shallow zone by chance.","Rival explanation 2: the fleet used the springs for drinking water only, and the beds were chosen for the oysters, not the water.","Rival explanation 3: lower salinity from the springs harms rather than helps pearl oysters, so the richest beds would lie away from the discharge."],"refs":["News of Bahrain. The land of two seas: the freshwater springs that made Bahrain.","Al-Noaimi, M. A. (2004). Development of water resources in Bahrain. PhD thesis, University of Plymouth.","UNESCO World Heritage Centre. Pearling, Testimony of an Island Economy (ref. 1364rev), inscribed 2012.","Altuntaş, L. (2022). British archaeologists unearth the 1,200-year-old man-made island. Arkeonews, 13 February 2022.","Johannes, R. E. (1980). The ecological significance of the submarine discharge of groundwater. Marine Ecology Progress Series 3, 365–373.","Moore, W. S. (2010). The effect of submarine groundwater discharge on the ocean. Annual Review of Marine Science 2, 59–88."],"ic":"spark","era":"?","te":"Did Fresh Water Feed the Pearl Beds?","ta":"هل غذّى الماء العذب مغاصات اللؤلؤ؟","se":"The sea springs, the three oyster beds and an island built around a spring all lie in the same waters off Muharraq. Did fresh water from the sea floor make the pearls? Nobody has tested this yet.","sa":"تقع العيون البحرية والمغاصات الثلاث وجزيرة بُنيت حول عين في المياه نفسها قبالة المحرق. هل صنع الماءُ العذب الصاعد من قاع البحر اللؤلؤَ؟ لم يختبر أحد ذلك بعد.","na":"العيون البحرية ومغاصات اللؤلؤ","t0":1900,"t1":90,"spec":{"bas":"link","key":"sea-spring","sty":"ai-sea-spring","spot":{"en":"Born from a coincidence of places on this map. The record of the submarine springs says they are most numerous off Muharraq; the Pearling Path record puts the three oyster beds in the same northern waters; and Al Sayah Island, built around a sea spring, is buried in pearl-oyster shell. Three records, one patch of sea.","ar":"وُلدت الفكرة من تطابق أماكن على هذه الخريطة. سجل العيون البحرية يقول إنها أكثر ما تكون قبالة المحرق، وسجل طريق اللؤلؤ يضع المغاصات الثلاث في المياه الشمالية نفسها، وجزيرة السياح المبنية حول عين بحرية مدفونة تحت أصداف المحار. ثلاثة سجلات، وبقعة واحدة من البحر."},"lk":[["submarine-springs","Fresh water in 5 m of sea, most numerous off Muharraq","ماء عذب على عمق 5 أمتار في البحر، أكثره قبالة المحرق"],["pearling-path","Three oyster beds in the northern waters","ثلاث مغاصات في المياه الشمالية"],["al-sayah-island","An island built around a sea spring, covered in oyster shell","جزيرة بُنيت حول عين بحرية، تغطيها أصداف المحار"],["dammam-aquifer-springs","The aquifer that feeds both the land and the sea springs","الخزان الذي يغذي عيون البر والبحر معًا"]],"pred":{"en":"Radon and salinity surveys will show submarine discharge concentrated under or beside the three oyster beds, and oyster density will be highest where discharge is strongest.","ar":"ستُظهر مسوح الرادون والملوحة تدفقًا جوفيًا بحريًا متركزًا تحت المغاصات الثلاث أو بجانبها، وستكون كثافة المحار أعلى حيث التدفق أقوى."},"test":{"en":"Map submarine groundwater discharge off Muharraq with radon-222 tracers and salinity profiles, then compare it with the positions and productivity of the hayrat and with the historical spring sites.","ar":"رسم خريطة التدفق الجوفي البحري قبالة المحرق بمتتبعات الرادون-222 وقياسات الملوحة، ثم مقارنتها بمواقع الهيرات وإنتاجيتها وبمواقع العيون التاريخية."},"wrong":{"en":"If the richest oyster banks show no groundwater signal, or if oysters do worse where fresh water rises, the idea fails.","ar":"إذا لم تُظهر أغنى المغاصات أي إشارة لمياه جوفية، أو إذا كان المحار أسوأ حالًا حيث يصعد الماء العذب، تسقط الفكرة."}}},{"id":"spec-tree-of-life-water","n":"The Rus Formation and the Tree of Life","c":"speculative","s":"Cross-category hypothesis","lat":26.01,"lng":50.57,"p":"Eocene rock, living tree","dr":"1582 – present (test window: present day)","el":"","desc":"A connection between records on this map. The Tree of Life, a mesquite 9.75 m tall planted around 1582, stands alone on a low hill about 6 km from Jebel Dukhan with no visible spring; its roots may reach 50 m to groundwater. The Rus Formation, exposed in the breached core of the Bahrain anticline, holds the island's lowest aquifer, and the Interior Basin is the only place on the island with meaningful local recharge. The idea is that the tree sits where water stored in the Rus or lower Dammam limestones, recharged in the basin, lies within reach of its roots, and that the 500-year-old pottery found beside it in 2010 marks people who once used the same water.","sig":"If true, the tree would be a living outcrop of the island's deepest freshwater, the 1582 planting and the pottery would record a well or seep that has since vanished, and the tree's future would depend on the same aquifer that abstraction has been draining since the 1950s.","ex":"","conf":"Untested AI speculation (connection between published records)","ev":"Tree of Life: 9.75 m tall, planted c. 1582 by tree-ring and soil analysis, 6 km from Jebel Dukhan, roots may reach 50 m, 500-year-old pottery found beside it in October 2010. Jebel Dukhan: the Rus Formation, 67 m of chalky dolomitic limestone with chert and anhydrite, is exposed in the core of the anticline, overlain by the Dammam Formation. Interior Basin: the only place with meaningful local recharge of groundwater. Rus Formation outcrop: the unit that holds the island's lowest aquifer, 26.5 m section logged in 2023.","an":"Phillips (1963) recorded mesquite (Prosopis) roots at 53 m depth in an open-pit mine near Tucson, Arizona, the deepest roots ever measured; Canadell et al. (1996) compiled rooting depths worldwide and confirmed that desert phreatophytes routinely reach groundwater tens of metres down.","val":"Sample xylem water from the tree and compare its stable isotopes (δ18O, δ2H) with rain, shallow sand moisture and water from the Rus and Dammam aquifers; run electrical resistivity tomography across the hill to look for a freshwater lens or a conductive root zone; drill a shallow piezometer beside the tree and log the water table and its chemistry through the year; re-examine the 2010 pottery context for a well or cistern.","disc":["Rival explanation 1: the tree lives on moisture held in the sand and on winter rain, with no connection to any aquifer.","Rival explanation 2: the tree reaches a perched saline or brackish lens, not the Rus or Dammam aquifers, and tolerates it.","Rival explanation 3: the tree was planted and watered by people at a camp or well, and survived once established on sand moisture alone."],"refs":["Mancuso, S. (2020). The Incredible Journey of Plants. Other Press.","Willis, R. P. (1967). Geology of the Arabian Peninsula: Bahrain. U.S. Geological Survey Professional Paper 560-E.","Amer, A., Skyttä, P., Ojala, A. & Heilimo, E. (2023). New insights into the depositional facies of the Early Eocene Rus Formation, Bahrain. Marine and Petroleum Geology 154, 106338.","Phillips, W. S. (1963). Depth of roots in soil. Ecology 44(2), 424.","Canadell, J., Jackson, R. B., Ehleringer, J. R., Mooney, H. A., Sala, O. E. & Schulze, E.-D. (1996). Maximum rooting depth of vegetation types at the global scale. Oecologia 108, 583–595."],"ic":"spark","era":"?","te":"What Does the Tree of Life Drink?","ta":"ماذا تشرب شجرة الحياة؟","se":"A 400-year-old tree lives in bare desert 6 km from the rocks that hold the island's deepest aquifer. Is it drinking from the Rus Formation? Nobody has tested this yet.","sa":"شجرة عمرها 400 عام تعيش في صحراء جرداء على بعد 6 كيلومترات من الصخور التي تحوي أعمق خزان في الجزيرة. هل تشرب من تكوين الرس؟ لم يختبر أحد ذلك بعد.","na":"تكوين الرس وشجرة الحياة","t0":444,"t1":0,"spec":{"bas":"link","key":"tree-water","sty":"ai-tree-water","spot":{"en":"Born from a coincidence of places on this map. The Tree of Life record says the tree stands 6 km from Jebel Dukhan with no visible spring and roots that may reach 50 m. The Jebel Dukhan and Interior Basin records say the Rus Formation, which holds the island's lowest aquifer, is exposed there, and that the basin is the only place with local recharge. One tree, one aquifer, six kilometres.","ar":"وُلدت الفكرة من تطابق أماكن على هذه الخريطة. سجل شجرة الحياة يقول إنها تقف على بعد 6 كيلومترات من جبل الدخان بلا عين ظاهرة وبجذور قد تبلغ 50 مترًا. وسجلا جبل الدخان والحوض الداخلي يقولان إن تكوين الرس، الذي يحوي أدنى خزان في الجزيرة، مكشوف هناك، وإن الحوض هو المكان الوحيد الذي يتغذى فيه الخزان محليًا. شجرة واحدة، وخزان واحد، وستة كيلومترات."},"lk":[["tree-of-life","A mesquite alive since 1582 with no visible spring","شجرة مسكيت حية منذ 1582 بلا عين ظاهرة"],["jebel-dukhan","The Rus Formation exposed in the core of the anticline, 6 km away","تكوين الرس مكشوف في قلب الطيّة، على بعد 6 كيلومترات"],["interior-basin","The only place on the island with local recharge","المكان الوحيد في الجزيرة الذي يتغذى فيه الخزان محليًا"],["rus-formation-outcrop","The unit that holds the island's lowest aquifer","الوحدة التي تحوي أدنى خزان في الجزيرة"]],"pred":{"en":"The tree's xylem water will match the isotopic signature of the Rus or lower Dammam aquifer rather than rain or sand moisture, and geophysics will show a freshwater zone within 50 m of the surface under the hill.","ar":"سيطابق ماء نسيج الخشب في الشجرة البصمة النظائرية لخزان الرس أو الدمام السفلي لا بصمة المطر أو رطوبة الرمل، وسيُظهر المسح الجيوفيزيائي منطقة ماء عذب في حدود 50 مترًا تحت التلّ."},"test":{"en":"Compare stable isotopes of the tree's xylem water with the aquifers, rain and sand moisture; image the ground under the hill with electrical resistivity; drill a piezometer and log the water table for a year.","ar":"مقارنة النظائر المستقرة لماء نسيج الخشب في الشجرة بالخزانات والمطر ورطوبة الرمل، وتصوير ما تحت التلّ بالمقاومة الكهربائية، وحفر بئر رصد وتسجيل منسوب الماء عامًا كاملًا."},"wrong":{"en":"If the xylem water matches recent rain or shallow sand moisture, or if no freshwater zone exists within reach of the roots, the idea fails.","ar":"إذا طابق ماء نسيج الخشب المطرَ الحديث أو رطوبة الرمل السطحية، أو لم توجد منطقة ماء عذب في متناول الجذور، تسقط الفكرة."}}},{"id":"spec-bh-markh-islet","n":"The Islet of the Dugong Hunters","c":"speculative","s":"Cross-category hypothesis","lat":25.97,"lng":50.55,"p":"Late Ubaid / mid-Holocene","dr":"c. 5000–3800 BC (test window)","el":"","desc":"A connection between records on this map. The sediments under the King Fahd Causeway record a late Pleistocene lowstand when the whole Gulf was emptied, and the Flandrian flooding that refilled it about 6,000 years ago. Al Markh, a Late Stone Age fishing camp of about 5000–3800 BC now 1,200 m from the shore, may have been an islet when sea level stood up to 4 m higher; its earlier phase holds small sparid fish and pearl oyster, while its later phase adds larger fish and the first bones of dugong, which were common. Today the shallow Gulf of Salwa to the south, with extensive Halodule and Halophila seagrass, holds the world's second-largest dugong population. The idea is that the change between Al Markh's two phases records seagrass meadows spreading over newly flooded shallows, so that the camp's food remains are a dated record of the Gulf of Salwa ecosystem taking shape.","sig":"If true, Al Markh would date the arrival of the seagrass and dugong system that still defines the waters south of Bahrain, give a deep-time baseline for the population counted since 1986, and show how food remains in a camp can be read as a record of sea-level change.","ex":"","conf":"Untested AI speculation (connection between published records)","ev":"Al Markh: a low mound 6 km south of Zallaq and 1,200 m from the shore, excavated by Michael Roaf in 1975; 6,896 flint pieces and pottery dated to about 3800 BC; an earlier phase with sparid fish, pearl oyster and fire pits, and a later phase with larger fish and the first bones of goat, hare and dugong, which were common; sea level thought up to 4 m higher, so the mound may have been a seasonal islet. King Fahd Causeway shelf: the Gulf was emptied entirely in the late Pleistocene regression and reflooded about 6,000 years ago. Gulf of Salwa: waters mostly under 10 m deep with extensive Halodule and Halophila seagrass; a herd of 674 dugongs counted in March 1986.","an":"Méry et al. (2009) described a Neolithic mound built of dugong bones on the small island of Akab in Umm al-Quwain, United Arab Emirates, and read it as a ritual site, showing that coastal communities of the Arabian Gulf were hunting dugongs from islands thousands of years ago; Beech (2010) reviewed the archaeological evidence for dugong exploitation in the Gulf from prehistory to the present.","val":"Core the ground around Al Markh to find the old shoreline and date beach shells, to test whether the mound was an islet; identify and measure the fish bones of both phases and compare their species with today's seagrass and reef fish; look for seagrass-dwelling molluscs and foraminifera in dated cores from the shallows between Al Markh and the Gulf of Salwa; radiocarbon-date the dugong bones directly.","disc":["Rival explanation 1: the seagrass was already there, and the later people simply had new skills, nets or boats that let them take dugongs.","Rival explanation 2: a change of sea level moved the shoreline and put the camp beside different waters, so the shift reflects geography, not new meadows.","Rival explanation 3: the two phases belong to different groups or different seasons of use, and the change in food says nothing about the sea itself."],"refs":["Roaf, M. (1976). Excavations at Al Markh, Bahrain. Proceedings of the Seminar for Arabian Studies 6.","Beech, M. J. (2010). Mermaids of the Arabian Gulf: archaeological evidence for the exploitation of dugongs from prehistory to the present. Liwa 2(3), 3–18.","Darwish, A. & Conley, C. D. (1990). Pleistocene-Holocene sedimentation and diagenesis along the King Fahd causeway between Saudi Arabia and Bahrain. Journal of King Abdulaziz University: Earth Sciences 3(1), 63–79.","Preen, A. (2004). Distribution, abundance and conservation status of dugongs and dolphins in the southern and western Arabian Gulf. Biological Conservation 118, 205–218.","Méry, S., Charpentier, V., Auxiette, G. & Pelle, E. (2009). A dugong bone mound: the Neolithic ritual site on Akab in Umm al-Quwain, United Arab Emirates. Antiquity 83(321), 696–708."],"ic":"spark","era":"?","te":"Did a Rising Sea Bring the Dugongs to Al Markh?","ta":"هل جاء البحر الصاعد بالأطوم إلى المرخ؟","se":"A Stone Age camp south of Zallaq switched from small fish to dugongs, on the edge of the shallows where the world's second-largest dugong population lives today. Did the camp see the seagrass arrive? Nobody has tested this yet.","sa":"انتقل مخيم من العصر الحجري جنوب الزلاق من الأسماك الصغيرة إلى الأطوم، على حافة المياه الضحلة التي يعيش فيها اليوم ثاني أكبر تجمع للأطوم في العالم. هل شهد المخيم وصول الأعشاب البحرية؟ لم يختبر أحد ذلك بعد.","na":"جزيرة صيادي الأطوم","t0":7025,"t1":5800,"spec":{"bas":"link","key":"bhmarkh","sty":"ai-bhmarkh","spot":{"en":"Born from a coincidence of records on this map. The Al Markh record says dugong bones become common only in the site's later phase, and that the mound may once have been an islet in a higher sea. The causeway record dates the refilling of the Gulf to about 6,000 years ago, and the Gulf of Salwa record shows the seagrass and dugongs that fill the nearby shallows today. Three records, one coast, and a change of menu.","ar":"وُلدت الفكرة من تطابق سجلات على هذه الخريطة. سجل المرخ يقول إن عظام الأطوم لا تكثر إلا في المرحلة المتأخرة من الموقع، وإن التلّ ربما كان يومًا جزيرة صغيرة في بحر أعلى. وسجل الجسر يؤرخ امتلاء الخليج من جديد بنحو 6,000 عام مضت، وسجل خليج سلوى يُظهر الأعشاب البحرية والأطوم التي تملأ المياه الضحلة القريبة اليوم. ثلاثة سجلات، وساحل واحد، وتغيّر في الطعام."},"lk":[["al-markh","A Stone Age camp where dugong bones appear in the later phase","مخيم من العصر الحجري تظهر فيه عظام الأطوم في المرحلة المتأخرة"],["causeway-pleistocene","The Gulf refilled by the sea about 6,000 years ago","الخليج يمتلئ بالبحر من جديد قبل نحو 6,000 عام"],["gulf-of-salwa-dugongs","Seagrass shallows and the world's second-largest dugong population","مياه ضحلة بالأعشاب البحرية وثاني أكبر تجمع للأطوم في العالم"]],"pred":{"en":"Cores around Al Markh will show a shoreline that made the mound an islet, and seagrass-dwelling shells and foraminifera will appear in the nearby shallows at about the same time as dugong bones become common in the site's later phase.","ar":"ستُظهر العيّنات اللبّية حول المرخ خط شاطئ جعل التلّ جزيرة صغيرة، وستظهر الأصداف والمنخربات التي تعيش في الأعشاب البحرية في المياه الضحلة القريبة في الوقت نفسه تقريبًا الذي تكثر فيه عظام الأطوم في المرحلة المتأخرة من الموقع."},"test":{"en":"Date the old shoreline around Al Markh; identify the fish and shellfish of both phases; core the shallows between the site and the Gulf of Salwa and date the first seagrass fauna; date the dugong bones directly.","ar":"تأريخ خط الشاطئ القديم حول المرخ، وتحديد أنواع الأسماك والمحار في المرحلتين، وأخذ عيّنات لبّية من المياه الضحلة بين الموقع وخليج سلوى وتأريخ أول كائنات الأعشاب البحرية، وتأريخ عظام الأطوم مباشرة."},"wrong":{"en":"If seagrass fauna in the nearby shallows turns out to be much older than the site's earlier phase, or if the fish of both phases are the same seagrass species, the idea fails.","ar":"إذا تبين أن كائنات الأعشاب البحرية في المياه الضحلة القريبة أقدم بكثير من المرحلة المبكرة للموقع، أو أن أسماك المرحلتين هي أنواع الأعشاب البحرية نفسها، تسقط الفكرة."}}},{"id":"spec-bh-hypocolius-palms","n":"The Hypocolius and the Lost Springs","c":"speculative","s":"Cross-category hypothesis","lat":26.19,"lng":50.54,"p":"Living today / groundwater decline","dr":"1952 – present (test window: present day)","el":"","desc":"A connection between records on this map. The grey hypocolius roosts at Maqabah, in 0.157 km² of abandoned date-palm scrub, and feeds by day in palm gardens elsewhere on the island; the site's record calls it a reminder of the vanished northern palm belt. The date groves of the north were fed by springs of the Dammam aquifer, such as Ain Adhari; abstraction rose from about 63 million m³ in 1952 to 188 million m³ in 2001, and most springs no longer flow naturally. At Tubli Bay, most of the original mangrove, intertidal and date-palm habitat has been lost since 1975. The idea is that the bird's winter range in Bahrain is set by the spring-fed palm belt, so that its shrinking habitat is, at one remove, a measure of the falling aquifer.","sig":"If true, the hypocolius would be a living indicator of groundwater in the north of the island, and protecting it would mean protecting not only a small patch of scrub but the water and the gardens around it.","ex":"","conf":"Untested AI speculation (connection between published records)","ev":"Maqabah: 0.157 km² of abandoned date-palm scrub at 2–5 m elevation, about 10 km from Manama, with palms covering about 60%; the main roost in Bahrain of the grey hypocolius, a passage and wintering bird that feeds by day in palm gardens elsewhere on the island; Important Bird Area last assessed in 1994; no legal protection. Dammam Aquifer: Ain Adhari fed the date groves; abstraction 68% above inflow by 2001; most springs no longer flow naturally and almost 70% of usable groundwater has been lost or salinised. Tubli Bay: most mangrove, intertidal and date-palm habitats lost since 1975 to reclamation, roads and sewage, especially on the northern shore.","an":"Stromberg, Tiller and Richter (1996) showed along the San Pedro River in Arizona that falling groundwater changes riparian vegetation, with depth to the water table strongly shaping which water-dependent plant communities persist; the habitat that animals rely on in dry lands can therefore track the aquifer beneath it.","val":"Map where the hypocolius feeds by day through one winter, by counts along transects or by tracking; overlay those gardens on historical maps of springs and date groves from the 1950s and on modern groundwater levels and irrigation sources; compare any long-term roost counts with the abstraction record; measure the food available in watered and dry palm gardens.","disc":["Rival explanation 1: the roost depends on shelter and freedom from disturbance, not on water, and the birds would stay wherever old palms stand.","Rival explanation 2: the number of birds arriving is set by conditions on their breeding grounds and migration routes outside Bahrain.","Rival explanation 3: palm gardens were lost mainly to building and roads, so housing, not the aquifer, explains any decline."],"refs":["Key Biodiversity Areas Partnership. Maqabah (KBA factsheet 8018), BirdLife International.","Al-Noaimi, M. A. (2004). Development of water resources in Bahrain: a combined approach of supply–demand analysis. PhD thesis, University of Plymouth.","BirdLife International. Key Biodiversity Area factsheet: Tubli Bay (KBA 8017).","Stromberg, J. C., Tiller, R. & Richter, B. (1996). Effects of groundwater decline on riparian vegetation of semiarid regions: the San Pedro, Arizona. Ecological Applications 6(1), 113–131."],"ic":"spark","era":"?","te":"Is a Bird Following the Water Table?","ta":"هل يتبع طائرٌ منسوبَ الماء الجوفي؟","se":"A rare bird roosts in the last palm scrub of the north, where the springs that watered the date groves have mostly stopped. Is the hypocolius following the water table? Nobody has tested this yet.","sa":"يبيت طائر نادر في آخر بقايا النخيل في الشمال، حيث توقفت أغلب العيون التي كانت تسقي بساتين النخيل. هل يتبع البُلبُل الرمادي منسوب الماء الجوفي؟ لم يختبر أحد ذلك بعد.","na":"البُلبُل الرمادي والعيون المفقودة","t0":74,"t1":0,"spec":{"bas":"link","key":"bhhypocolius","sty":"ai-bhhypocolius","spot":{"en":"Born from a coincidence of records on this map. The Maqabah record says the hypocolius roosts in old palm scrub, feeds in palm gardens and is a reminder of the vanished northern palm belt. The Dammam aquifer record says the springs that fed the date groves have mostly stopped, and the Tubli Bay record lists date-palm habitat among what has been lost since 1975. A bird, a palm belt and an aquifer, all shrinking together.","ar":"وُلدت الفكرة من تطابق سجلات على هذه الخريطة. سجل مقابة يقول إن البُلبُل الرمادي يبيت في بقايا نخيل قديمة، ويقتات في بساتين النخيل، وإن الموقع تذكير بحزام النخيل الشمالي الذي اختفى. وسجل خزان الدمام يقول إن العيون التي كانت تسقي البساتين توقفت في أغلبها، وسجل خليج توبلي يعدّ موائل النخيل بين ما فُقد منذ 1975. طائر، وحزام نخيل، وخزان جوفي، كلها تتقلص معًا."},"lk":[["maqabah-hypocolius","The main roost of the grey hypocolius, in old palm scrub","المرقد الرئيسي للبُلبُل الرمادي، في بقايا نخيل قديمة"],["dammam-aquifer-springs","The springs that fed the date groves, now mostly dry","العيون التي كانت تسقي بساتين النخيل، وقد جفّ أغلبها"],["tubli-bay","Date-palm habitat lost since 1975","موائل نخيل فُقدت منذ 1975"]],"pred":{"en":"The hypocolius will feed by day mainly in palm gardens that still have water, and the gardens the birds no longer use will be those whose springs or wells have failed.","ar":"سيقتات البُلبُل الرمادي نهارًا في الغالب في بساتين نخيل ما زال فيها ماء، والبساتين التي هجرتها الطيور ستكون تلك التي جفت عيونها أو آبارها."},"test":{"en":"Map the birds' daytime feeding sites for a winter and compare them with the water source of each garden, with historical spring and grove maps, and with modern groundwater levels.","ar":"رسم خريطة لمواقع اقتيات الطيور نهارًا طوال شتاء كامل، ومقارنتها بمصدر الماء في كل بستان، وبخرائط العيون والبساتين التاريخية، وبمناسيب الماء الجوفي الحالية."},"wrong":{"en":"If the hypocolius feeds as readily in dry or abandoned palms as in watered gardens, or if its feeding sites show no relation to water at all, the idea fails.","ar":"إذا كان البُلبُل الرمادي يقتات في النخيل الجاف أو المهجور كما يقتات في البساتين المسقية، أو إذا لم تُظهر مواقع اقتياته أي صلة بالماء، تسقط الفكرة."}}},{"id":"spec-bh-solstice-temples","n":"The Solstice Temples of Dilmun","c":"speculative","s":"Cross-site hypothesis","lat":26.213,"lng":50.481,"p":"Early Dilmun","dr":"c. 2500–1800 BC (test window)","el":"","desc":"A connection between records on this map. The temple at Saar, in a planned Early Dilmun town of about 2050–1800 BC, is believed to have marked the summer solstice. A few kilometres to the north, the columned temple at Diraz, of the 3rd or early 2nd millennium BC, has a central hall with two rows of columns, a square base read as an altar or statue base, and a small square cella to the east; its architecture differs from both Mesopotamian temples and Barbar. The three superimposed temples of Barbar, from about 3000 to 2000 BC, stand within the same few kilometres. The idea is that, despite their different designs, Dilmun's temples shared a common orientation to the sun at the solstices, so that one rule of the sky ran alongside a religion otherwise known mainly for its water cult.","sig":"If true, it would show that Dilmun religion had an astronomical side alongside the cult of Enki and fresh water, test whether the different plan of Diraz hides a shared tradition, and offer a way to predict the orientation of temples not yet found.","ex":"","conf":"Untested AI speculation (connection between published records)","ev":"Saar: a planned town of about 2.5 ha, c. 2050–1800 BC, with houses along streets and a temple believed to have marked the summer solstice; excavated 1977–1979 and by the London–Bahrain Archaeological Expedition in 1990–1999. Diraz Temple: about 700 m² surviving; a central hall with two rows of cylindrical columns about 120 cm across, nine still standing, a square base read as an altar or statue base and a small square cella to the east; dated by finds to the 3rd or early 2nd millennium BC; column architecture unlike Mesopotamian temples or Barbar. Barbar: three temples built one above the other, c. 3000–2000 BC, with a spring shrine read as the abzu of Enki.","an":"Hoskin (2001) measured the orientations of large numbers of prehistoric tombs and temples around the western Mediterranean and found that monuments of one culture tend to face a consistent range of directions, often related to sunrise or the sun's path, a pattern that only becomes visible when many sites are measured together.","val":"Survey the main axis of the Saar temple, the hall and cella at Diraz, and each phase of Barbar; compute the azimuths of sunrise and sunset at the solstices and equinoxes for about 2000 BC, allowing for the low horizon of the north of the island; test statistically whether the axes cluster more closely than chance would allow; compare each temple's axis with the street grid and water features around it.","disc":["Rival explanation 1: each temple simply follows the street grid or the lie of the land, and any shared direction is a coincidence of town planning.","Rival explanation 2: the temples are oriented to their water, the spring or channel, rather than the sky, as the Barbar spring shrine might suggest.","Rival explanation 3: the solstice reading at Saar is itself mistaken, and Dilmun temples have no common orientation at all."],"refs":["Crawford, H., Killick, R. & Moon, J. (eds.) (1997). The Dilmun Temple at Saar. Kegan Paul International.","Bahrain Authority for Culture and Antiquities. Diraz Temple (cultural tourism destinations page).","Andersen, H. H. & Højlund, F. (2003). The Barbar Temples. Jutland Archaeological Society.","Hoskin, M. (2001). Tombs, Temples and Their Orientations: A New Perspective on Mediterranean Prehistory. Ocarina Books, Bognor Regis."],"ic":"spark","era":"?","te":"Did Dilmun Build Its Temples to the Sun?","ta":"هل بنت دلمون معابدها نحو الشمس؟","se":"The temple at Saar is believed to mark the summer solstice. Did the temples of Diraz and Barbar, a few kilometres away, face the same sun? Nobody has tested this yet.","sa":"يُعتقد أن معبد سار كان يحدد الانقلاب الصيفي. فهل واجه معبدا الدراز وباربار، على بعد كيلومترات قليلة، الشمسَ نفسها؟ لم يختبر أحد ذلك بعد.","na":"معابد الانقلاب الشمسي في دلمون","t0":4525,"t1":3775,"spec":{"bas":"link","key":"bhsolstice","sty":"ai-bhsolstice","spot":{"en":"Born from a coincidence of records on this map. The Saar record says its temple is believed to have marked the summer solstice. The Diraz record describes a temple of the same age with its cella to the east, and stresses that its design differs from Barbar. Three temples within a few kilometres, one with a known link to the sky, and nothing on this map to say whether the other two share it.","ar":"وُلدت الفكرة من تطابق سجلات على هذه الخريطة. سجل سار يقول إن معبدها يُعتقد أنه كان يحدد الانقلاب الصيفي. وسجل الدراز يصف معبدًا من العصر نفسه تقع حجرته الصغيرة في الشرق، ويؤكد أن تصميمه يختلف عن باربار. ثلاثة معابد في بضعة كيلومترات، لأحدها صلة معروفة بالسماء، ولا شيء على هذه الخريطة يقول إن كان الآخران يشاركانه إياها."},"lk":[["saar-settlement","A temple believed to have marked the summer solstice","معبد يُعتقد أنه كان يحدد الانقلاب الصيفي"],["diraz-temple","A columned temple with its cella to the east","معبد بأعمدة تقع حجرته الصغيرة في الشرق"],["barbar-temple","Three temples over a spring, a few kilometres away","ثلاثة معابد فوق عين، على بعد كيلومترات قليلة"]],"pred":{"en":"The main axes of the Saar, Diraz and Barbar temples will cluster around the direction of sunrise or sunset at a solstice for about 2000 BC, more closely than chance would allow, whatever their plans.","ar":"ستتجمع المحاور الرئيسية لمعابد سار والدراز وباربار حول اتجاه شروق الشمس أو غروبها في أحد الانقلابين لنحو 2000 ق.م، أقرب مما تسمح به الصدفة، مهما اختلفت تصاميمها."},"test":{"en":"Measure the axes of all three temples and each of their phases, compute solstice and equinox azimuths for about 2000 BC, and compare them with the street grid and the springs.","ar":"قياس محاور المعابد الثلاثة وكل مراحلها، وحساب اتجاهات الانقلابين والاعتدالين لنحو 2000 ق.م، ومقارنتها بشبكة الشوارع وبالعيون."},"wrong":{"en":"If the axes point in unrelated directions, or match the street grid or the springs better than the sun, the idea fails.","ar":"إذا أشارت المحاور إلى اتجاهات لا علاقة بينها، أو طابقت شبكة الشوارع أو العيون أكثر من الشمس، تسقط الفكرة."}}}],"stops":[]})}catch(e){console.error("D",String(e).slice(0,80))}
